{{{{{}}{}{}{}{}{{}}}}}{}{{{{{}}{}{}{}{}{{}}}}}{{}}{}{}{}{}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 5.9 0 L 13.87 -15.94}{fill:none} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
\lxSVG@closescope }}}\hbox{{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} 
{{}}{}{{}}{{}}{{{{}}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{}}{{}}}{{{{}}{}{}{}{}{{}}}}
}{{{{}}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{}}{{}}}{{{{}}{}{}{}{}{{}}}}
}{{}{}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{{}}
{}{}{}{{{}}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{}}}
{{{}}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{{}}}
{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{{}}{}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{}{}{}{}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M -0.79 -1.37 C 0.49 -9.33 4.71 -14.51 12.25 -17.38}{fill:none} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
\lxSVG@closescope }}}\hbox{{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} {
{}{}{}}{}{{}}
{{{{{}}{}{}{}{}{{}}}}}{}{{{{{}}{
{}{}}{}{}{{}{}}}}}{{}}{}{}{}{}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 15.75 -43.31 L 15.75 -23.88}{fill:none} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
\lxSVG@closescope }}}
\lxSVG@closescope {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p4.4" class="ltx_p">Each graph produced by the differential has one new structural edge, which is taken first in the ordering; the relative ordering of the remaining edges is preserved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For all <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\neq(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\neq(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> there is an <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\cong H^{\bullet}(X_{g,n},\delta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\cong H^{\bullet}(X_{g,n},\delta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This isomorphism at the level of cohomology is achieved through a zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of complexes, relating the row of Deligne’s weight spectral sequence that computes <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> to the graph complex <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology of such commutative graph complexes is far from fully understood. Nevertheless, a partial understanding of this graph cohomology is enough to yield new results about the unstable cohomology of <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> in weight 2, just as in weight 0. For <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we express the weight 2 compactly supported cohomology of <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of the weight 0 compactly supported cohomology of <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\leq g</annotation></semantics></math>, as follows. Let</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\bullet,\bullet}:=\textstyle{\bigwedge^{2}}\Big(\textstyle{\bigoplus_{g,k}}W_{0}H^{k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\Big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\bullet,\bullet}:=\textstyle{\bigwedge^{2}}\Big(\textstyle{\bigoplus_{g,k}}W_{0}H^{k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">bigraded so that an element of <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\wedge W_{0}H^{k^{\prime}}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\wedge W_{0}H^{k^{\prime}}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},1})</annotation></semantics></math> is in degree <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g+g^{\prime},k+k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g+g^{\prime},k+k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{as}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{as}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the antisymmetric part of a <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-representation <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>. By convention, we set <math id="S1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,2}):=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,2}):=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, there is an isomorphism of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H_{c}^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\ \cong\ \mathbb{V}_{g,k-3}\oplus W_{0}H^{k-3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,2})_{as}\oplus\mathbb{V}_{g-1,k-4}\oplus W_{0}H^{k-4}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g-2,2})_{as}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H_{c}^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\ \cong\ \mathbb{V}_{g,k-3}\oplus W_{0}H^{k-3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,2})_{as}\oplus\mathbb{V}_{g-1,k-4}\oplus W_{0}H^{k-4}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g-2,2})_{as}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the Hodge structure on <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H_{c}^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H_{c}^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Tate; this follows from Deligne’s weight spectral sequence associated to <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\subset\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\subset\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, which abuts to <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\bullet}H_{c}^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\bullet}H_{c}^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the fact that <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> is pure Tate for all <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>; see §<a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3. Deligne’s weight spectral sequence ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, below. The Hodge structure on <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H_{c}^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H_{c}^{k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore obtained from the <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector space description above by tensoring with <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. For any variety or Deligne-Mumford stack <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{1}H^{*}_{c}(X)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{1}H^{*}_{c}(X)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, such as <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the subspace <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}H_{c}^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}H_{c}^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> is an extension of <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H_{c}^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H_{c}^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H_{c}^{*}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H_{c}^{*}(X)</annotation></semantics></math>, in the category of mixed Hodge structures. For <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X={\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X={\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, one might naturally expect that this extension is trivial. To prove this, it would be enough to show that <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{2}H_{c}^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{2}H_{c}^{*}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is the mixed Hodge realization of a mixed Tate motive over <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{Z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{Z}}</annotation></semantics></math>, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, (1.6.11)]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">To understand the geometric meaning of the first two summands in this theorem, let <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{g}^{\circ}\subset\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{g}^{\circ}\subset\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be the locally closed substack parametrizing <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-nodal curves. We have a natural identification</p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{k}_{c}(D^{\circ}_{g})\cong\mathbb{V}_{g,k}\oplus W_{0}H^{k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,2})_{as}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{k}_{c}(D^{\circ}_{g})\cong\mathbb{V}_{g,k}\oplus W_{0}H^{k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g-1,2})_{as}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Cup product with the first Chern class of the normal bundle followed by the push-forward for the open inclusion <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\iota" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ι</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\iota</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\circ}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\circ}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> in the boundary <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}\smallsetminus{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}\smallsetminus{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and the coboundary map <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> for the excision sequence give</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}(D^{\circ}_{g})\xrightarrow{c_{1}\smile}\gr_{2}H^{\bullet+2}_{c}(D^{\circ}_{g})\xrightarrow{\iota_{*}}\gr_{2}H^{\bullet+2}_{c}\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\xrightarrow{\delta}\gr_{2}H^{\bullet+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">c</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">⌣</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mover><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>D</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>∘</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">ι</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msub></mover><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝛿</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}(D^{\circ}_{g})\xrightarrow{c_{1}\smile}\gr_{2}H^{\bullet+2}_{c}(D^{\circ}_{g})\xrightarrow{\iota_{*}}\gr_{2}H^{\bullet+2}_{c}\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\xrightarrow{\delta}\gr_{2}H^{\bullet+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p7.3" class="ltx_p">We expect that the geometric content of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> is that this composition is injective and the image accounts for the first two summands in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>. As a first step toward seeing this, note that <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a smooth differential form that extends to the boundary. This pulls back to the normalization of <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and hence acts on every page of the weight spectral sequence abutting to <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> (cf. the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> in Section <a href="#S8.SS4" title="8.4. The cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                
                J
                ⋆
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  1
                  ,
                  n ‣ 8. Direct summands with marked points ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a>). This action can be interpreted graphically by following our zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms. We do not have an analogous expected geometric interpretation for the other two summands. For a graphical illustration of the simplest non-trivial cohomology classes produced by Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, see Section <a href="#S3.SS4" title="3.4. Example of a nontrivial cocycle in 
          
            
              
              
                
                GK
                
                  
                    
                    
                    
                  
                  
                    g
                    ,
                    n
                  
                
              
              2 ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">For applications, we will use only subspaces of <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\bullet,\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\bullet,\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> that arise from the images of known classes under pullback to the universal curve <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\hookrightarrow W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\hookrightarrow W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Recall that <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{2g}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{2g}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonvanishing for <math id="S1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>, and its dimension grows at least exponentially with <math id="S1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>; the proof relies on connections with Grothendieck-Teichmüller theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and a deep result of Francis Brown <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Here, we show furthermore that <math id="S1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonvanishing for <math id="S1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, and its dimension again grows at least exponentially with <math id="S1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>; see Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>. It follows that the dimensions of <math id="S1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+k+3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+k+3}</annotation></semantics></math> grow at least exponentially with <math id="S1.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S1.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{2g^{\prime}+k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime}})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{2g^{\prime}+k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime}})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S1.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. Combining Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> with what is currently known about <math id="S1.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and applying Poincaré duality, we have the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The following unstable rational cohomology groups are nonzero:</span></p>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_start_2_columns"></div>
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=9</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 11;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 11;</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-9}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-9}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8;</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-11}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-11}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=10</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 12</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-12}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-12}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=9</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-14}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-14}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=15</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 17</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-15}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-15}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=14</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 16</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(7)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-16}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-16}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=13</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S1.I3.i7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=15</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and </span><math id="S1.I3.i7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 17;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 17;</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(8)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-18}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-18}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=17" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=17</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 19;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 19;</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(9)</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I3.i9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-19}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-19}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> for </span><math id="S1.I3.i9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=16</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I3.i9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 18</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I3.i9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_end_2_columns"></div>
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In each case, the dimension of <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{4g-k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{4g-k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> grows at least exponentially with <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, this gives the first infinite collections of nonvanishing odd cohomology groups on <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Only a few sporadic examples were known previously, such as <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{5}({\mathcal{M}}_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{5}({\mathcal{M}}_{4})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, and <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{15}({\mathcal{M}}_{6})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>15</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{15}({\mathcal{M}}_{6})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{23}({\mathcal{M}}_{8})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>23</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{23}({\mathcal{M}}_{8}),</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{27}({\mathcal{M}}_{10})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>27</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{27}({\mathcal{M}}_{10})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. For the proof of Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>, see Section <a href="#S2.SS7" title="2.7. The support of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                ∙
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    X
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        g
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For context, let us recall that <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is fully understood in the stable range, consisting of degrees up to <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\lfloor g/3\rfloor" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌊</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌋</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\lfloor g/3\rfloor</annotation></semantics></math>, where it agrees with the polynomial ring on the <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-classes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. The Euler characteristic computations of Harer and Zagier show that the dimension of the unstable cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> grows super-exponentially with <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. This is far larger than the tautological subring generated by the <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>-classes, which is nonzero precisely in the even degrees up to <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-4</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. The problem of understanding the rest of the cohomology of <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, starting with the degrees in which it is zero and nonzero, is highlighted in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, Section 9]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Until recently, there were only two instances of cohomology groups that were known to be nonzero due to the presence of non-tautological classes: <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6}({\mathcal{M}}_{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6}({\mathcal{M}}_{3})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{5}({\mathcal{M}}_{4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{5}({\mathcal{M}}_{4})</annotation></semantics></math>, each of which is 1-dimensional. It was previously conjectured by Kontsevich and independently by Church, Farb, and Putman that <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5-k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5-k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\gg 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≫</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\gg 0</annotation></semantics></math>, when <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. This is true for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> for virtual cohomological dimension reasons and for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math> by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. The main result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> disproved the conjecture for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> shows that the conjecture is false in nine more cases, namely for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\{3,4,6,7,9,10,11,13,14\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\{3,4,6,7,9,10,11,13,14\}</annotation></semantics></math>. See Table <a href="#S1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, at the end of the introduction, for a summary of what is now known about the vanishing and non-vanishing of <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5-k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5-k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> uses only the subspace of <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{\bullet,\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{\bullet,\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> that comes from <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\hookrightarrow W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\hookrightarrow W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Explicit computations show that this inclusion is an isomorphism for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&lt;7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&lt;7</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=8</annotation></semantics></math>, the Euler characteristic formulas in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> show that it is not surjective, but we do not know in which degrees the cokernel is supported. Similarly, <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,2})_{as}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,2})_{as}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g&lt;7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g&lt;7</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=8</annotation></semantics></math>, its Euler characteristic is nonzero but we do not know in which degrees it is supported. By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, any future improvement in our understanding of <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,2})_{as}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,2})_{as}</annotation></semantics></math> will yield a corresponding improvement in our understanding of <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. For an inspiring sense of what one might reasonably hope or expect to be true about <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, Conjecture 1 and Table 2]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> we do not have an analogue of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> expressing <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Nevertheless, we produce injections that prove nonvanishing and lower bounds on dimensions in many cases. We begin with <math id="S1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that the pullback <math id="S1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\colon\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\to\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\colon\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\to\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math> is injective, as is the composition</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\xrightarrow{\pi^{*}}\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\xrightarrow{\psi\wedge}\gr_{k+2}H^{\bullet+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">∗</mo></msup></mover><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">ψ</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∧</mo></mrow></mover><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\xrightarrow{\pi^{*}}\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\xrightarrow{\psi\wedge}\gr_{k+2}H^{\bullet+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p10.2" class="ltx_p">because further composition with the Gysin push-forward is multiplication by <math id="S1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g-2</annotation></semantics></math>. For weight <math id="S1.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we strengthen this by showing that <math id="S1.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\wedge" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∧</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\wedge</annotation></semantics></math> is itself injective:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Multiplication by the <math id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-class at the marking yields an injection</span></p>
<table id="S1.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\wedge\colon W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\to\gr_{2}H^{\bullet+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">∧</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\wedge\colon W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\to\gr_{2}H^{\bullet+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Furthermore, the image of the injection</span></p>
<table id="S1.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\colon\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\to\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\colon\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\to\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm6.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">intersects trivially with that of (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, we show that a quasi-isomorphic subcomplex of <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be split into several direct summands, as detailed in Section <a href="#S6" title="6. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. For some of these summands one may evaluate the cohomology explicitly in terms of known data or weight 0 cohomology. The summands whose cohomology can be computed correspond, in a sense to be made precise below, to graphs of the form</p>
<table id="S1.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
