<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1. </span>A summary of vanishing and nonvanishing results for the rational cohomology groups <math id="S1.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5-k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5-k}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. Black and dark gray boxes denote previously known and new nonvanishing groups, respectively. The white boxes denote groups that are known to vanish, and the light gray boxes denote those that are as yet unknown.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Notation and conventions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We work over the rational numbers <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>. All vector spaces are understood to be <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces, and likewise all homology and cohomology groups are taken with <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-coefficients. If <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite-dimensional vector space with the action of a finite group <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we identify the invariant subspace <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>V</mi><mi>G</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{G}</annotation></semantics></math> with the coinvariant space <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, by averaging over the group action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a graded vector space <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and an integer <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V[k]</annotation></semantics></math> be the graded vector space obtained by shifting all degrees by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., the degree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> part of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is the degree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j-k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j-k</annotation></semantics></math> part of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V[k]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V[k]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">When working with graded vector spaces we use the standard Koszul sign convention.
That is, for <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> graded vector spaces the isomorphism <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes W\cong W\otimes V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes W\cong W\otimes V</annotation></semantics></math> sends a tensor product <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\otimes w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\otimes w</annotation></semantics></math> of homogeneous elements <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in W</annotation></semantics></math> of degrees <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|v|,|w|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|v|,|w|</annotation></semantics></math> to the element <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(-1)^{|v||w|}w\otimes v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(-1)^{|v||w|}w\otimes v</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, let <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathrm{Hom}(V,V^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathrm{Hom}(V,V^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathrm{Hom}(W,W^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathrm{Hom}(W,W^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> be two linear maps between graded vector spaces, of homogeneous degree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|f|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|f|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|g|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|g|</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Then we define the linear map <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\otimes g\in\mathrm{Hom}(V\otimes V^{\prime},W\otimes W^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\otimes g\in\mathrm{Hom}(V\otimes V^{\prime},W\otimes W^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, such that, for homogeneous <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in W</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f\otimes g)(v\otimes w)=(-1)^{|g||v|}f(v)\otimes g(w)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f\otimes g)(v\otimes w)=(-1)^{|g||v|}f(v)\otimes g(w).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The phrase “differential graded” is abbreviated dg. We follow cohomological grading conventions, so all differentials have degree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math> unless stated otherwise. The Koszul sign rule will be particularly important when we consider the tensor product of dg vectors space <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,d_{V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,d_{V})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(W,d_{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>W</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(W,d_{W})</annotation></semantics></math>. The differential on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\otimes W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\otimes W</annotation></semantics></math> is then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{V\otimes W}:=d_{V}\otimes\id_{W}+\id_{V}\otimes d_{W}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>id</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{V\otimes W}:=d_{V}\otimes\id_{W}+\id_{V}\otimes d_{W}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">When no confusion seems possible, we denote this by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{V}+d_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{V}+d_{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the Koszul sign convention understood.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will consider many instances of spaces that depend on a genus <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and a number of marked points <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, among them the moduli spaces <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the dg vector spaces <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we follow the usual convention of omitting this from the notation, e.g., we write <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g}:=X_{g,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g}:=X_{g,0}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Quasi-isomorphisms and acyclicity</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A morphism of dg vector spaces is a quasi-isomorphism if it induces an isomorphism on cohomology, and a dg vector space <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,d)</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic if its cohomology vanishes. We will make repeated use of the following elementary sufficient criteria for morphisms of dg vector spaces to be quasi-isomorphisms, and for dg vector spaces to be acyclic.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,d)</annotation></semantics></math> be a dg vector space with <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> a dg subspace. Suppose there is a decomposition of graded vector spaces <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=V_{0}\oplus V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=V_{0}\oplus V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projection onto <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D=\pi_{1}\circ d:V_{0}\to V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D=\pi_{1}\circ d:V_{0}\to V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker D\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker D\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}\oplus\im D\subset V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}\oplus\im D\subset V</annotation></semantics></math> are dg subvector spaces. Moreover,</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is surjective, then </span><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker D\to V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker D\to V</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a quasi-isomorphism, and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is injective then
</span><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\to V/(V_{0}\oplus\im D)=\coker D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\to V/(V_{0}\oplus\im D)=\coker D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a quasi-isomorphism.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, if <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism, then <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,d)</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The differential <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is a sum of three pieces <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
