<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">The fact that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{2}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{2}=0</annotation></semantics></math> translates into the three equations</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{0}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{0}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{1}D+Dd_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{1}D+Dd_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">It follows that <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ker D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ker D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}\oplus\im D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}\oplus\im D</annotation></semantics></math> are dg subspaces of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, we show that if <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism then <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V,d)</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V</annotation></semantics></math> be a cocycle, and write <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=x_{0}+x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=x_{0}+x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in V_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in V_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> is a cocycle, we have <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}x_{0}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}x_{0}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Dx_{0}+d_{1}x_{1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Dx_{0}+d_{1}x_{1}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Dx_{0}=-d_{1}x_{1}=-d_{1}DD^{-1}x_{1}=Dd_{0}D^{-1}x_{1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Dx_{0}=-d_{1}x_{1}=-d_{1}DD^{-1}x_{1}=Dd_{0}D^{-1}x_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">Applying <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math> to both sides shows that <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}=d_{0}D^{-1}x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}=d_{0}D^{-1}x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=dD^{-1}x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=dD^{-1}x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a coboundary, as required.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Statements (1) and (2) follow by applying this criterion for acyclicity to <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V/\ker D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V/\ker D</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}\oplus\im D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>im</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}\oplus\im D</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>Deligne’s weight spectral sequence</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The natural starting point for understanding the weight-graded compactly supported cohomology of a smooth variety or Deligne-Mumford stack with a given normal crossings compactification is the Poincaré dual of Deligne’s weight spectral sequence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, §3.2]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, Example 3.5]</cite>. We briefly recall this construction in the special case of the Deligne-Mumford compactification of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by the space of stable <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-marked curves <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is naturally stratified by the topological type, which is encoded combinatorially in the dual graph of the curve. Each stratum thus corresponds to a stable <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-marked graph <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., a graph with <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> legs labeled <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\ldots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\ldots,n</annotation></semantics></math> in which each vertex <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is labeled by a non-negative integer <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g_{v}+n_{v}\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g_{v}+n_{v}\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the valence of the vertex <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and such that <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=h^{1}(\Gamma)+\sum_{v}g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=h^{1}(\Gamma)+\sum_{v}g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each such stable <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-marked graph <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, the locus of curves with dual graph <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of the natural gluing map</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}\colon{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}\colon{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}:=\prod_{v\in V(\Gamma)}{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma}:=\prod_{v\in V(\Gamma)}{\mathcal{M}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}.</annotation></semantics></math> This gluing extends naturally to <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}:=\prod_{v}\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∏</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}:=\prod_{v}\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\Gamma}:=\xi_{\Gamma}(\prod_{v}\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∏</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\Gamma}:=\xi_{\Gamma}(\prod_{v}\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})</annotation></semantics></math> be the image of this extension; it is the closure of the locus of curves with dual graph <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. The normalization of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the smooth and proper Deligne-Mumford stack</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{\Gamma}=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}/\Aut(\Gamma)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{\Gamma}=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}/\Aut(\Gamma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Note that the codimension of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of edges in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Let</p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}^{j}:=\bigsqcup_{|E(\Gamma)|=j}\widetilde{D}_{\Gamma}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨆</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}^{j}:=\bigsqcup_{|E(\Gamma)|=j}\widetilde{D}_{\Gamma}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">The image of a small neighborhood of a point in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in precisely <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> analytic branches of the boundary divisor <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}\smallsetminus{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}\smallsetminus{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The monodromy action on these branches defines a local system of rank <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> whose determinant is denoted <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page of the weight spectral sequence for <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed naturally in terms of cohomology with coefficients in this local system:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{j,k}\cong H^{k}(\widetilde{D}^{j},\epsilon^{j})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{j,k}\cong H^{k}(\widetilde{D}^{j},\epsilon^{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Note that the branches of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> that contain the image of a small neighborhood of a point in <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{D}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{D}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> are naturally identified with the edges of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is trivialized by pullback to <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this way, the local system <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with the determinant of the permutation action of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}^{j,k}\cong\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=j}\big(H^{k}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}^{j,k}\cong\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=j}\big(H^{k}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.6" class="ltx_p">The spectral sequence degenerates at <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{k}H^{j+k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{k}H^{j+k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is canonically identified with <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> of the complex</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S2.E4.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:17.3pt;vertical-align:-10.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-103.9pt,5.7pt) scale(0.601796235896546,0.601796235896546) ;">
<p id="S2.E4.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.E4.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\cdots\to\bigoplus_{|\Gamma|=j-1}\big(H^{k}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\xrightarrow{d_{j-1}}\bigoplus_{|\Gamma|=j}\big(H^{k}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\xrightarrow{d_{j}}\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=j+1}\big(H^{k}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\to\cdots}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.710em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mover><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">j</mi></msub></mover><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊗</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\cdots\to\bigoplus_{|\Gamma|=j-1}\big(H^{k}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\xrightarrow{d_{j-1}}\bigoplus_{|\Gamma|=j}\big(H^{k}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\xrightarrow{d_{j}}\bigoplus_{|E(\Gamma)|=j+1}\big(H^{k}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma})\otimes\det E(\Gamma)\big)^{\Aut(\Gamma)}\to\cdots}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.7" class="ltx_p">All of the data in this spectral sequence is neatly encoded in the language of modular operads <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, Example 3.9]</cite>. More precisely, the cohomology groups <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> naturally form a modular cooperad, whose Feynman transform evaluated at <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum, over all weights <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, of the complex (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.3. Deligne’s weight spectral sequence ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). Using the Künneth decomposition, one may encode generators as linear combinations of graphs <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> whose vertices <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are labeled with elements of <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k_{v}}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k_{v}}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v}k_{v}=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v}k_{v}=k</annotation></semantics></math>. The result is now known as the Getzler-Kapranov graph complex and denoted <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>; see, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, Section 6.1]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4. </span>Modular cooperads and Feynman transform</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We briefly recall the notion of modular cooperads and their Feynman transforms, and refer the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> for details. A stable <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}</annotation></semantics></math>-module is a collection of dg vector spaces <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}=\{{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}=\{{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, with each <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with an action of <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a stable <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}</annotation></semantics></math>-module.
For a stable graph <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> we may define the tensor product</p>
<table id="S2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\otimes_{\Gamma}{\mathcal{P}}:=\otimes_{v\in V(\Gamma)}{\mathcal{P}}(g_{v},n_{v})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo rspace="0em">:=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes_{\Gamma}{\mathcal{P}}:=\otimes_{v\in V(\Gamma)}{\mathcal{P}}(g_{v},n_{v}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Aut(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Aut(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> acts naturally on <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\otimes_{\Gamma}{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\otimes_{\Gamma}{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>. A modular cooperad is a stable <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}</annotation></semantics></math>-module together with a morphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)\to\otimes_{\Gamma}{\mathcal{P}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)\to\otimes_{\Gamma}{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">for every stable graph of genus <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> legs, satisfying suitable compatibility relations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To a modular cooperad <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, one naturally associates the Feynman transform <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a modular cooperad whose underlying stable <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝕊</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}</annotation></semantics></math>-module is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)\cong\bigoplus_{[\Gamma]}\left(\bigotimes_{\Gamma}{\mathcal{P}}\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes|E(\Gamma)|}\right)_{\Aut(\Gamma)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></munder><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></munder><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)\cong\bigoplus_{[\Gamma]}\left(\bigotimes_{\Gamma}{\mathcal{P}}\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes|E(\Gamma)|}\right)_{\Aut(\Gamma)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the sum is over isomorphism classes of stable graphs of genus <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> legs, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E(\Gamma)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E(\Gamma)|</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of edges of <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, and the action of the automorphism group is diagonal on <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigotimes_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>⨂</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigotimes_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes|E(\Gamma)|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes|E(\Gamma)|}</annotation></semantics></math> by permutation of factors. Elements of <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> can be understood as isomorphism classes of graphs whose vertices are decorated by suitable elements of <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In cases where <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> has no differential, the differential on <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> has two pieces,</p>
<table id="S2.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\delta_{split}+\delta_{loop}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\delta_{split}+\delta_{loop}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.3" class="ltx_p">The piece <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> splits vertices similar to (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), using the cooperadic composition of <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}</annotation></semantics></math> on decorations.
Similarly, <math id="S2.SS4.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{loop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{loop}</annotation></semantics></math> creates a loop at a vertex</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

