<p id="S2.SS4.p3.4" class="ltx_p">using the modular cooperadic map
<math id="S2.SS4.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)\to{\mathcal{P}}(g-1,n+2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{P}}(g,n)\to{\mathcal{P}}(g-1,n+2)</annotation></semantics></math>
on the decoration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5. </span>The Getzler-Kapranov graph complex</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The cohomology groups <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> of the Deligne-Mumford compactified moduli spaces <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> assemble into a modular cooperad that we shall denote <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})</annotation></semantics></math>. We consider the Feynman-transform <math id="S2.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})</annotation></semantics></math> of this modular cooperad.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An element of <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear combination of stable <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-marked graphs of genus <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> whose vertices <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> are decorated by elements of <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})</annotation></semantics></math>. As recalled in the preceding section, the differential has two parts</p>
<table id="S2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=\delta_{split}+\delta_{loop}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=\delta_{split}+\delta_{loop}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The dg vector spaces <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})(g,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})(g,n)</annotation></semantics></math> inherit the weight grading from <math id="S2.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where the weight of a graph is the sum of the degrees of its vertex decorations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The weight <math id="S2.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> Getzler-Kapranov graph complex is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}^{k}_{g,n}:=\gr_{k}\mathcal{F}H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})(g,n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}^{k}_{g,n}:=\gr_{k}\mathcal{F}H(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})(g,n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}^{k}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}^{k}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is also naturally identified with (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In 2.3. Deligne’s weight spectral sequence ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>), the weight <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> row in on the <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page of the weight spectral sequence for <math id="S2.SS5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\subset\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}\subset\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and hence there is a canonical isomorphism</p>
<table id="S2.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}\mathsf{GK}^{k}_{g,n}\cong\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}\mathsf{GK}^{k}_{g,n}\cong\gr_{k}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.6. </span>A nonvanishing result in weight 0</h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we state and prove Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, a nonvanishing and exponential growth result for <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. This proposition, combined with Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and the results on <math id="S2.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, gives the nonvanishing statements in Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the full commutative graph complex studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. The elements of <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> are (possibly infinite) formal <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-linear combinations of isomorphism classes of connected graphs with vertices of any valence. It is a dg Lie algebra with a combinatorially defined bracket, and the differential <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is the bracket with a single edge. The subspace <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> spanned by stable graphs is a direct summand, and the restriction of <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is exactly <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math>. The cohomology of <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}})=H(\mathsf{GC}_{2})\oplus\bigoplus_{k\geq 1}\mathbb{Q}[L_{4k+1}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}})=H(\mathsf{GC}_{2})\oplus\bigoplus_{k\geq 1}\mathbb{Q}[L_{4k+1}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a loop of <math id="S2.SS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> edges, in which every vertex has valence 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, Proposition 3.4]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the cohomological degree of a graph in <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> edges is <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e-2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e-2g</annotation></semantics></math>. There is an extra differential <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.SS6.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math>, given by bracket with a loop edge, studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every class in <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is represented by a <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-cocycle <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.Thmthm3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-cocycle representing a nontrivial class in <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathsf{GC}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathsf{GC}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We must show that there is some <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}-F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}-F</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-exact and <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F^{\prime}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F^{\prime}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. First, note that
<math id="S2.SS6.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta\nabla F=-\nabla\delta F=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\nabla F=-\nabla\delta F=0.</annotation></semantics></math>
Since <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes in negative degrees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>, we see that <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-exact, i.e., there is <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1}\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1}\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta F_{1}=\nabla F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta F_{1}=\nabla F</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta\nabla F_{1}=-\nabla\delta F_{1}=-\nabla^{2}F=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>δ</mi><mi>F</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\nabla F_{1}=-\nabla\delta F_{1}=-\nabla^{2}F=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Continuing in this way, the number of edges remains fixed and the genus increases by 1 at each step, so eventually we arrive at a sequence <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(F=F_{0},\ldots,F_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(F=F_{0},\ldots,F_{n})</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\delta F_{n}=\nabla F_{n-1}\quad\quad\mbox{ and }\nabla(F_{n})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta F_{n}=\nabla F_{n-1}\quad\quad\mbox{ and }\nabla(F_{n})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Then, since <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla</annotation></semantics></math>-acyclic in genus greater than 1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Corollary 3]</cite>, there is some <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla G_{n}=F_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla G_{n}=F_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, then we can replace <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{n-1}+\delta G_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{n-1}+\delta G_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to get a shorter sequence that satisfies (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Repeating the process, we arrive at such a sequence with <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Choose <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla G=F_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla G=F_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="S2.SS6.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F^{\prime}=F+\delta G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F^{\prime}=F+\delta G</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> that <math id="S2.SS6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with the genus <math id="S2.SS6.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> part of the cohomology of the complex of commutative stable graphs <math id="S2.SS6.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and the dimension of <math id="S2.SS6.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{2g}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{2g}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> grows at least exponentially with <math id="S2.SS6.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The weight zero cohomology group <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero for <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, its dimension grows at least exponentially with <math id="S2.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us denote the genus <math id="S2.SS6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> part of <math id="S2.SS6.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS6.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GC}_{2}^{(g)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GC}_{2}^{(g)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The main result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> shows that
<math id="S2.SS6.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{2g+k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\cong H^{k}(\mathsf{GC}_{2}^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{2g+k}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\cong H^{k}(\mathsf{GC}_{2}^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall that <math id="S2.SS6.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally identified with the Grothendieck-Teichmüller Lie algebra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>. Using this identification, it follows from the main results of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> that <math id="S2.SS6.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> contains a free Lie subalgebra generated by classes <math id="S2.SS6.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{g}\in H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2}^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{g}\in H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2}^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math> for odd <math id="S2.SS6.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, (1.3)]</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We consider two linear maps induced by the Lie bracket:</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{3},\cdot]\colon H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})\to H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})\quad\quad\mbox{ and }\quad\quad[L_{5},\cdot]\colon H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})\to H^{3}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext> and </mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{3},\cdot]\colon H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})\to H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})\quad\quad\mbox{ and }\quad\quad[L_{5},\cdot]\colon H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})\to H^{3}(\mathsf{GC}_{2}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">The natural target of <math id="S2.SS6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[L_{5},\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[L_{5},\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS6.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{3}(\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{3}(\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}})</annotation></semantics></math>, but the image of any nontrivial class has genus greater than 1; by (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>), it must be in the summand <math id="S2.SS6.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{3}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{3}(\mathsf{GC}_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that the kernel of <math id="S2.SS6.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[L_{5},\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[L_{5},\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the kernel of <math id="S2.SS6.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{3},\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{3},\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math>. The proposition follows from the claim, since the free Lie algebra generated by <math id="S2.SS6.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS6.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{7}</annotation></semantics></math>, …has trivial intersection with the kernel of <math id="S2.SS6.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{3},\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{3},\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> and hence also with the kernel of <math id="S2.SS6.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[L_{5},\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[L_{5},\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math>. It remains to prove the claim.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.SS6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> be a cocyle of genus <math id="S2.SS6.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> representing a class in <math id="S2.SS6.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2}^{(g)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖢</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\mathsf{GC}_{2}^{(g)})</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose <math id="S2.SS6.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[L_{5},F]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[L_{5},F]</annotation></semantics></math> is exact, and write it as <math id="S2.SS6.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(\lambda)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(\lambda)</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.SS6.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝖿𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>conn</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in\mathsf{fGC}_{2,\mathrm{conn}}</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S2.SS6.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+1</annotation></semantics></math>. We must show that <math id="S2.SS6.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{3},F]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{3},F]</annotation></semantics></math> is exact. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, we may assume <math id="S2.SS6.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that there is a graph <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta+\nabla)(L_{5}+\gamma)=\sigma_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∇</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta+\nabla)(L_{5}+\gamma)=\sigma_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, Figure 2]</cite>). Let <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta:=[\gamma,F]-\nabla\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta:=[\gamma,F]-\nabla\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S2.SS6.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta=[L_{5}+\gamma,F]-(\delta+\nabla)\lambda," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ζ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∇</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta=[L_{5}+\gamma,F]-(\delta+\nabla)\lambda,</annotation></semantics></math>
and, using the assumption that <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla F=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla F=0</annotation></semantics></math>, it follows that</span></p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\delta+\nabla)\zeta=[\sigma_{3},F]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mo>∇</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\delta+\nabla)\zeta=[\sigma_{3},F].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p9.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">Note that <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ζ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> is of genus <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{3},F]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{3},F]</annotation></semantics></math> is of genus <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g+3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> leaves the genus invariant, while <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∇</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla</annotation></semantics></math> increases it by one.
Hence, comparing terms of like genus in (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>), we find that separately <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\zeta=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\zeta=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla\zeta=[\sigma_{3},F]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla\zeta=[\sigma_{3},F]</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> denote the vertex deletion operation introduced by Živković in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, Section 3]</cite>. Then <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla=\delta D-D\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∇</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla=\delta D-D\delta</annotation></semantics></math> (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, Proposition 3.3]</cite>) and hence</span></p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{3},F]=\nabla\zeta=(\delta D-D\delta)\zeta=\delta(D\zeta)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{3},F]=\nabla\zeta=(\delta D-D\delta)\zeta=\delta(D\zeta).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS6.p9.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS6.p9.3.1" class="ltx_text">This shows that <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\sigma_{3},F]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\sigma_{3},F]</annotation></semantics></math> is exact, as required, and proves the proposition.
We note that <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, Proposition 3.3]</cite> is stated in a version of the graph complex that also contains disconnected graphs. However, since the connected graphs span a direct summand with respect to <math id="S2.SS6.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, given that (<a href="#S2.E8" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) holds in the larger complex, it must also hold for the connected part.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.7. </span>The support of <math id="S2.SS7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(X_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(X_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we deduce bounds on the support of <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(X_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(X_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, using mixed Hodge theory and known vanishing statements for <math id="S2.SS7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. We also explain how Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> follows from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_cor">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graph cohomology <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(X_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(X_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is supported in degrees between <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max\{2g,2g-2+n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max\{2g,2g-2+n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3g-2+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3g-2+n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we must show that <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is supported in the indicated range of degrees. By Poincaré duality,
we have</span></p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\cong\gr_{6g-8+2n}H^{6g-6+2n-\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\vee}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0em">−</mo><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})\cong\gr_{6g-8+2n}H^{6g-6+2n-\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})^{\vee}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS7.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">To see the upper bound, recall that <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{\ast}H^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{\ast}H^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported in weights <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ast\leq 2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ast\leq 2k</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Hence the right hand side of (<a href="#S2.E9" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.7. The support of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                ∙
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    X
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        g
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) vanishes when <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bullet&gt;3g-2+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∙</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bullet&gt;3g-2+n</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the lower bound also follows from (<a href="#S2.E9" title="In Proof. ‣ 2.7. The support of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                ∙
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    X
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        g
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), since the virtual cohomological dimension (vcd) of <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-4+n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-4+n</annotation></semantics></math>. The lower bounds for <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> are similar; one uses that the vcds of <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4g-5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4g-5</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and that <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-3}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-3}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-5}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-5}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> both vanish <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we explain how Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> follows from Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS7.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We start by recalling that</span></p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{2g}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{2g}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonvanishing for <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=5</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>, by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonvanishing for <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 8</annotation></semantics></math>, by Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS7.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">In both cases the dimension grows exponentially with <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, we know that <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{27}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{10})\neq 0;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>27</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{27}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{10})\neq 0;</annotation></semantics></math> the corresponding graph cohomology computation is due to Bar-Natan and McKay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. For this proof only, let us set</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{W}_{0}:=\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{2g}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g});\quad\quad\mathbb{W}_{3}:=\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g});\quad\quad\mathbb{W}_{7}:=W_{0}H^{27}_{c}{\mathcal{M}}_{10}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>g</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>g</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>27</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>10</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{W}_{0}:=\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{2g}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g});\quad\quad\mathbb{W}_{3}:=\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g});\quad\quad\mathbb{W}_{7}:=W_{0}H^{27}_{c}{\mathcal{M}}_{10}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS7.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigwedge^{2}\mathbb{W}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{2}\mathbb{W}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> contributes to <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{g,2g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{g,2g}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>, and the dimension of this subspace grows exponentially with <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Considering the third summand in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, this shows that <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{2g+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{2g+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero for <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, and grows exponentially with <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding statement for <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> follows by Poincaré duality, since <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\cong H^{2g+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\cong H^{2g+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> for <math id="S2.SS7.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> for <math id="S2.SS7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-9}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-9}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is an immediate consequence of Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 2.4. ‣ 2.6. A nonvanishing result in weight 0 ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, since <math id="S2.SS7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-9}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\cong H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})^{\vee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-9}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\cong H^{2g+3}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})^{\vee}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The remaining cases of Corollary <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Corollary 1.4. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> are similar to the case of <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-8}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. The subspace <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{W_{0}}\wedge\mathbb{W}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>𝟘</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{W_{0}}\wedge\mathbb{W}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> contributes to <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+3}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=9</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>, and the dimension of this contribution grows exponentially with <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. Considering the third summand in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and applying Poincaré duality shows that <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-11}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-11}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is nonzero for <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 12</annotation></semantics></math>, and its dimension grows exponentially with <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding statements for <math id="S2.SS7.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-12}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-12}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> are proved similarly, using the first summand in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS7.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The next three cases, for <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-14}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-14}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-15}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-15}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-16}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-16}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> use the contributions of <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigwedge^{2}\mathbb{W}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{2}\mathbb{W}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{W}_{0}\wedge\mathbb{W}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{W}_{0}\wedge\mathbb{W}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+6}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+7}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The final two cases, for <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-18}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-18}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{4g-19}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{4g-19}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> are deduced similarly from the contribution of <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{W}_{3}\wedge\mathbb{W}_{7}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>𝕎</mi><mn>7</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{W}_{3}\wedge\mathbb{W}_{7}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS7.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{g,2g+10}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.8. </span>A vanishing result in weight 0.</h3>

<div id="S2.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main motivation is to use what is known in weight 0 to prove new results about the weight 2 cohomology <math id="S2.SS8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. However, it is worth noting that the information flows meaningfully in both directions, via Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>. For instance, applying basic results from mixed Hodge theory to the weight 2 cohomology, we deduce the following vanishing result in weight 0.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{3g-1}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,2})_{as}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{3g-1}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,2})_{as}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes for all <math id="S2.Thmthm6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Also,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{3g-2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\leq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>g</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{3g-2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\leq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, the graph complex <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g,2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g,2)}</annotation></semantics></math> computing <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,2})</annotation></semantics></math> (see section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) has no antisymmetric cohomology in the top degree, corresponding to trivalent graphs with 2 marked points. Furthermore the top degree cohomology of <math id="S2.SS8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{g}G^{(g,1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>g</mi></msub><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{g}G^{(g,1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to trivalent graphs, is at most one-dimensional.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">The antisymmetric part <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{3g-1}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g},2)_{as}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{3g-1}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g},2)_{as}</annotation></semantics></math> injects into <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{3g+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{3g+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, via the second summand in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>, and the Poincaré dual of <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{3g+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{3g+2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{6g-2}H^{3g-2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{6g-2}H^{3g-2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which vanishes because <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{*}H^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{*}H^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported in weights <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="*\leq 2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">*\leq 2k</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Similarly, the first summand in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> gives an injection from <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigwedge^{2}\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{3g-2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mo>⋀</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⨁</mo><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigwedge^{2}\bigoplus_{g}W_{0}H^{3g-2}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.SS8.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{g^{\prime}}\gr_{2}H^{3g^{\prime}-1}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{g^{\prime}}\gr_{2}H^{3g^{\prime}-1}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which vanishes for the same weight reasons.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS8.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">It is not known whether <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{3g-2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{3g-2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, or equivalently, whether every trivalent graph with one marked point is a coboundary in <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g,1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g,1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, for all <math id="S2.SS8.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Generators and relations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The decorated graphs that generate <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> have a particularly simple description. Each generator has an underlying stable graph of genus <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, and comes with a total ordering of the structural edges, i.e., the edges that are not incident to the external vertices labeled <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to the relation that reordering the structural edges is multiplication by the sign of the permutation. In addition, each vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is decorated by an element of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{k_{v}}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{k_{v}}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> are the genus and valence of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{v}k_{v}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{v}k_{v}=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})=\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})=\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{1}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for each moduli space <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}</annotation></semantics></math> attached to a vertex, any generator for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with decoration in <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})</annotation></semantics></math>, which we call the <em id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">special vertex</em>. After rescaling, we may assume all other vertices are decorated with the unit <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\in H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\in H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}})</annotation></semantics></math>; for simplicity, we omit these trivial decorations.
We can thus give a finite generating set for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by specifying a finite generating set for <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">tautological classes</span>: <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{a,A}=\delta_{g-a,A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{a,A}=\delta_{g-a,A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, for subsets <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\leq a\leq g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\leq a\leq g</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2a+|S|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2a+|S|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(g-a)+|A^{c}|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(g-a)+|A^{c}|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by graphs in which the special vertex <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is decorated by one of these tautological classes in <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, the set <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n_{v}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n_{v}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is implicitly identified with the set
set of half-edges incident to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, the tautological classes form a basis <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>; there are no further relations. However, for <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the tautological classes satisfy relations as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 2.2]</cite>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math> there is one relation:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\kappa=5\sum_{i=1}^{n}\psi_{i}+\delta_{irr}-5\sum_{A}\delta_{0,A}+7\sum_{A}\delta_{1,A}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>A</mi></munder><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>A</mi></munder><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\kappa=5\sum_{i=1}^{n}\psi_{i}+\delta_{irr}-5\sum_{A}\delta_{0,A}+7\sum_{A}\delta_{1,A}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>, there are <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> relations:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\psi_{i}-\sum_{|A|\geq 2}\delta_{0,A},\mbox{ \ \ \ \ \ \ and \ \ \ \ \ \ }12\psi_{i}=\delta_{irr}+12\sum_{i\in A}\delta_{0,A}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\psi_{i}-\sum_{|A|\geq 2}\delta_{0,A},\mbox{ \ \ \ \ \ \ and \ \ \ \ \ \ }12\psi_{i}=\delta_{irr}+12\sum_{i\in A}\delta_{0,A}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the relations are generated by:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\kappa=\sum_{A\notni x,y}(|A|-1)\,\delta_{0,A},\mbox{ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ }\psi_{i}=\sum_{i\in A\notni x,y}\delta_{0,A},\mbox{ \ \ \ \ \ \ and \ \ \ \ \ \ }\delta_{irr}=0\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∌</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>∌</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mtext> and </mtext><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=\sum_{A\notni x,y}(|A|-1)\,\delta_{0,A},\mbox{ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ }\psi_{i}=\sum_{i\in A\notni x,y}\delta_{0,A},\mbox{ \ \ \ \ \ \ and \ \ \ \ \ \ }\delta_{irr}=0\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.4" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\neq y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\neq y</annotation></semantics></math> run over elements of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. See also Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> for another presentation of <math id="S3.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_ex">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following is a generator in degree <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{8,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{8,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The inscribed numbers in vertices <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> representing the genera <math id="S3.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
