<div id="S3.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> also provides another embedding of <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the element of <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{V}_{8,16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕍</mi><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{V}_{8,16}</annotation></semantics></math> underlying the degree 19 cocycle of genus 8 depicted above gives rise to another non-trivial element of <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{20}({\mathcal{M}}_{9})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>20</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{20}({\mathcal{M}}_{9})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding cocycle in <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{9}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{9}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has approximately three times as many terms, and we do not draw it here. In the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> we do provide an explicit algorithm for obtaining the image of the corresponding cocycle in the quasi-isomorphic quotient <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{9}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>9</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{9}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{9}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mn>9</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{9}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>The quotient <math id="S3.SS5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{g,n}\subset\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{g,n}\subset\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the graded subspace spanned by graphs that have at least one vertex of positive genus.
Then define the quotient</p>
<table id="S3.Ex51" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex51.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}=\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}/(I_{g,n}+\delta I_{g,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mo>=</mo><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}=\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}/(I_{g,n}+\delta I_{g,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">In other words, <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by graphs all of whose vertices are of genus zero. Among those graphs we impose relations of the form <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{loop}\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{loop}\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> running over graphs with exactly one vertex of genus 1. The quotient <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> inherits the weight grading from <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the weight is the sum of the cohomological degrees of the vertex decorations. We denote the summand of weight <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by</p>
<table id="S3.Ex52" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{k}\subset\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{k}\subset\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">In particular, the weight 0 piece <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by graphs without loop edges, all of whose vertices have genus zero. After rescaling, we may assume that all vertex decorations are trivial, i.e., each decoration is the identity <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\in H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\in H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})</annotation></semantics></math>. One main result of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> is that the quotient <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{0}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{0}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{0}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{0}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism for all <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we prove an analogous result in weight 2. The weight 2 piece <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by graphs with all vertices of genus zero, and one special vertex decorated by a tautological generator for <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})</annotation></semantics></math>. We quotient by graphs with a loop edge at a non-special vertex. Furthermore, if there is a loop edge at the special vertex, then we impose the additional relations <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for sets of half-edges <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> that contain both half-edges of the loop. (Since <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})</annotation></semantics></math>, this also kills graphs labeled with <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> contains neither half-edge of the loop.)</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The quotient map <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism for all <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g,n</annotation></semantics></math> except <math id="S3.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=n=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=n=1</annotation></semantics></math> the right-hand complex is acyclic and the left-hand complex has one-dimensional cohomology, spanned by the class of a single-vertex graph with decoration <math id="S3.SS5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S3.Ex53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.Ex53.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="33.47" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-5.2px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 61.64 33.47" width="61.64"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,33.47) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(14.2,0) translate(0,9.07)"><g stroke-width="1.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.1 0 C 6.1 3.37 3.37 6.1 0 6.1 C -3.37 6.1 -6.1 3.37 -6.1 0 C -6.1 -3.37 -3.37 -6.1 0 -6.1 C 3.37 -6.1 6.1 -3.37 6.1 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;" stroke="#FFFFFF" stroke-width="0.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.1 0 C 6.1 3.37 3.37 6.1 0 6.1 C -3.37 6.1 -6.1 3.37 -6.1 0 C -6.1 -3.37 -3.37 -6.1 0 -6.1 C 3.37 -6.1 6.1 -3.37 6.1 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -2.76 -3.12)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.4em;--ltx-fo-height:0.45em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="6.24" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.24)" width="5.52"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex53.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle 1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -9.59 13.07)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.39em;--ltx-fo-height:0.49em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.1em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 6.73)" width="19.18"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex53.m1.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\scriptstyle\delta_{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">δ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.710em">r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\scriptstyle\delta_{irr}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 35.91 -4.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="6.92"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S3.Ex53.m1.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 6.79 0 L 31.02 0"></path></g></g></svg></span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6. </span>Resolutions of <math id="S3.SS6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.5. The quotient 
          
            
              ¯
              GK ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, we use the following presentation for <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. We believe this is well-known to experts. However, lacking a suitable reference, we include a short proof.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The cohomology group <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by boundary classes <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}</annotation></semantics></math> for subsets <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq|A|\leq n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq|A|\leq n-2</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-classes <math id="S3.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> modulo the relations</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">;</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}+\psi_{j}=\sum_{i\in A,j\in A^{c}}\delta_{0,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}+\psi_{j}=\sum_{i\in A,j\in A^{c}}\delta_{0,A}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">These classes generate <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})=2^{n-1}-\binom{n}{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">dim</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})=2^{n-1}-\binom{n}{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. The relations <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> are standard, and the quotient by these relations has dimension <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{n-1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{n-1}-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The remaining <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math> relations are independent, so it suffices to show that they hold in <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=3</annotation></semantics></math>, this is trivial. We proceed by induction on <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that (<a href="#S3.I1.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) holds in <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the universal curve. Then apply the pullback formulas <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\psi_{i}=\psi_{i}-\delta_{0,\{i,n+1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\psi_{i}=\psi_{i}-\delta_{0,\{i,n+1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A}+\delta_{0,A\cup\{n+1\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A}+\delta_{0,A\cup\{n+1\}}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Lemma 3.1]</cite> to deduce that (<a href="#S3.I1.i2" title="item 2 ‣ Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) holds in <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following proposition is an immediate consequence of Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is an <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant short exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex54" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex54.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\bigoplus_{1\leq i&lt;j\leq n}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}\to\Big(\bigoplus_{1\leq i\leq n}\mathbb{Q}\psi_{i}\Big)\oplus\bigoplus_{1\in A}\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,A}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\bigoplus_{1\leq i&lt;j\leq n}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}\to\Big(\bigoplus_{1\leq i\leq n}\mathbb{Q}\psi_{i}\Big)\oplus\bigoplus_{1\in A}\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,A}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n})\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in which the first arrow is given by <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}\mapsto\psi_{i}+\psi_{j}-\sum_{i\in A,j\in A^{c}}\delta_{0,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}\mapsto\psi_{i}+\psi_{j}-\sum_{i\in A,j\in A^{c}}\delta_{0,A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that here we identify <math id="S3.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action is given by permuting all labels and setting <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}=E_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}=E_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S3.SS6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\not\in A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\not\in A</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Filtration by the number of vertices</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main idea of the proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.5. The quotient 
          
            
              ¯
              GK ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> is to use the filtration on <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by the number of vertices, and look at the associated spectral sequence. The associated graded of this filtration <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>gr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{GK}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{GK}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., we kill the part of the differential splitting vertices.
As discussed in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. The filtered map of filtered complexes ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, this complex splits into subcomplexes that are direct sums of tensor products of complexes associated to single vertices. We begin by studying these single vertex complexes</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g,n}=\bigoplus_{h}\big(H^{\bullet}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes h}\big)_{\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mi>h</mi></munder><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g,n}=\bigoplus_{h}\big(H^{\bullet}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes h}\big)_{\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the wreath product <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by permuting the last <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2h</annotation></semantics></math> marked points and the factors <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes h}</annotation></semantics></math>, introducing signs via the Koszul sign rule, see section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Notation and conventions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. The weight grading on <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the cohomological degree in <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the complex decomposes into a direct sum of weight graded pieces. We denote the piece of weight <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g,n}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g,n}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>; in this paper, we focus on the cases <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>The single vertex complex in weight zero</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In weight zero, we have</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(V_{g,n}^{0})=\begin{cases}\mathbb{Q}&amp;\text{for $g=0$, $n\geq 3$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>ℚ</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mtext>, </mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(V_{g,n}^{0})=\begin{cases}\mathbb{Q}&amp;\text{for $g=0$, $n\geq 3$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To see this, note that</p>
<table id="S4.Ex55" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\big(H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes h}\big)_{\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}=\begin{cases}\mathbb{Q}&amp;\text{for $h\in\{0,1\}$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mi>ℚ</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\big(H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes h}\big)_{\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}=\begin{cases}\mathbb{Q}&amp;\text{for $h\in\{0,1\}$}\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and the differential on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{g,n}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{g,n}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is nontrivial when <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is positive.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One can deduce from (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In 4.1. The single vertex complex in weight zero ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) that <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{0}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{0}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{0}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{0}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism. However, we do not give the argument now. Instead, we proceed directly to the weight two case and merely remark that the weight zero case can be handled by a simplified version of the argument in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. The filtered map of filtered complexes ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, filtering by the number of vertices and using (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In 4.1. The single vertex complex in weight zero ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) in place of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. Thus, the methods presented here give an alternative approach to the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>)<span id="S4.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graph cohomology <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="H(G^{(g,n)})=H(\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(G^{(g,n)})=H(\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{0})</annotation></semantics></math> is identified with the weight zero part <math id="S4.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> of the compactly supported cohomology of the open moduli space of curves.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>The single vertex complex in weight 2</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.5. The quotient 
          
            
              ¯
              GK ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> will combine (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In 4.1. The single vertex complex in weight zero ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) with the following analogous statement in weight 2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and all <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> one has</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex56" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(V_{g,n}^{2})=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(V_{g,n}^{2})=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> one has</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex57" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(V_{1,n}^{2})\cong\mathbb{Q}\delta_{irr}\oplus\Big(H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}/\xi^{*}H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n})\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(V_{1,n}^{2})\cong\mathbb{Q}\delta_{irr}\oplus\Big(H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}/\xi^{*}H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n})\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, the differential on <math id="S4.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We write <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> in the form:</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.E17.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:11.5pt;vertical-align:-3.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(6.5pt,-0.2pt) scale(1.04352778365824,1.04352778365824) ;">
<p id="S4.E17.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.E17.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-1,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-2,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-3,n})_{\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to\cdots." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-1,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-2,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-3,n})_{\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to\cdots.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the action of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is understood to include a sign that accounts for the permutation of the tensor factors <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes h}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S4.E15" title="In 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>). To make this concrete, recall that <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the tautological classes <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{a,A}=\delta_{g-a,A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{a,A}=\delta_{g-a,A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math>, with relations for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> as discussed in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Then the action of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by a signed permutation action on this set of tautological generators, as follows. One may think of each generator as a graph with a single internal vertex of genus <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-h</annotation></semantics></math> incident to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> loop edges and <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> marked legs, equipped with a decoration by a tautological generator for <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})</annotation></semantics></math>. Then each copy of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by exchanging the half-edges in a loop (without sign). The quotient map to <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the permutation of the loop edges, and the induced permutation on generators is twisted by the sign of this <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>-action.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As a vector space <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{2}_{g,n}=\bigoplus_{h}H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})_{\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mi>h</mi></msub><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{2}_{g,n}=\bigoplus_{h}H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})_{\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the following classes</span></p>
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, with </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j\not\in\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j\not\in\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\{1,2\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\{1,2\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{a,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{a,A}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, for </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\{0,1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\{0,1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, the complex (<a href="#S4.E17" title="In 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) vanishes beyond <math id="S4.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Any other tautological generator for <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g-h,n+2h})</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved by an element of <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{h}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that acts by a transposition on the set of loop edges.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for <math id="S4.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, there are no nontrivial relations among the above generators for <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., all relations are induced by isomorphisms of marked and decorated graphs. In this case, we can see that the complex is acyclic by constructing a null-homotopy as follows. Depict boundary classes and <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-classes by marked edges and half-edges, respectively, as in Section <a href="#S3" title="3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Thus the generators of <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are depicted by graphs that have either one or two vertices. Say that a vertex <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S4.SS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">active</em> if either <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{v}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{v}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> or if there is a loop attached to <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that each vertex has at most one loop attached, since otherwise there is an odd automorphism interchanging two loops.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For each loop edge, there is a natural contraction, in which the loop edge is deleted and the genus of the attached vertex is increased by 1. The orientation on the resulting graph is determined as follows: after reordering, we may assume that the contracted loop was first in the loop order, and then the remaining edges keep their induced ordering. The null homotopy takes the sum of all such contractions, divided by the number of active vertices.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math> there is nothing to prove. When <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, additional arguments are required. For simplicity, we give complete proofs for <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>; the cases where <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> are similar. Throughout the remainder of the proof, we use <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> to denote an arbitrary subset of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to restrictions as specifically indicated. For instance, “<math id="S4.SS2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S},\ 1\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S},\ 1\in S</annotation></semantics></math>” refers to the <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> elements of the form <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> that contains <math id="S4.SS2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for <math id="S4.SS2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">The complex <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{2}_{3,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{2}_{3,n}</annotation></semantics></math> has the form</span></p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{3,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+6})_{\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{3,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+6})_{\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 2.10]</cite> we know that <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{3,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{3,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is injective. We now check exactness at the remaining places, working from right to left. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,u^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,u^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the pairs of markings added to <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> at each step. We claim that <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+6})_{\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+6})_{\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis consisting of the classes</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex58" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime},t\}},\ 1\in S." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime},t\}},\ 1\in S.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p7.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p7.3.1" class="ltx_text">This can be seen by taking
coinvariants of the resolution in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>; each <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is stabilized by an element of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that acts by a simple transposition on the set of 3 loops, and hence the coinvariants of <math id="S4.SS2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{i,j}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{i,j}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> vanish.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, recall that <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+4})</annotation></semantics></math> is freely generated by boundary classes, which are permuted by <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Taking coinvariants, we see that <math id="S4.SS2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis consisting of:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex59" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime}\}};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime},t\}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime}\}};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime},t\}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Working with respect to these bases, the same chain homotopy argument used for <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> shows that (<a href="#S4.E18" title="In Proof of Proposition  for 
            
              
                =
                g
                3
              
            
          . ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) is exact at <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+6})_{\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+6})_{\mathbb{S}_{3}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and at <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It remains to check that it is exact at <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We prove this when <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> by counting dimensions and computing the Euler characteristic; the argument for <math id="S4.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> is similar.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis given by <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n},\psi_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{1},\ldots,\psi_{n},\psi_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr}</annotation></semantics></math> together with:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex60" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S4.Ex60.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:12pt;vertical-align:-5.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-28.2pt,1.1pt) scale(0.847752953707951,0.847752953707951) ;">
<p id="S4.Ex60.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.Ex60.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{1,S};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{2,S};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{1,S\cup\{s\}},\ 1\in S." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{1,S};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{2,S};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{1,S\cup\{s\}},\ 1\in S.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">The dimension of <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{3,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{3,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\cdot 2^{n}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\cdot 2^{n}+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Counting the bases above shows that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex61" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(V^{2}_{3,n})=(2\cdot 2^{n}+1)-(4\cdot 2^{n}+2^{n-1})+(3\cdot 2^{n}-1)-2^{n-1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(V^{2}_{3,n})=(2\cdot 2^{n}+1)-(4\cdot 2^{n}+2^{n-1})+(3\cdot 2^{n}-1)-2^{n-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">which is <math id="S4.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for <math id="S4.SS2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">The complex <math id="S4.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{2,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{2,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> has the form</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex62" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p11.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p11.2.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Theorem 2.10]</cite>, we know that the first arrow is injective. We proceed to prove exactness at the remaining two places, working from right to left.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Say <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,s^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,s^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are the two pairs of markings added at each step. First, we claim that <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis consisting of:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime}\}},\ 1\in S;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime}\}},\ 1\in S;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">To see this, consider the <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-coinvariants of the resolution of <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})</annotation></semantics></math> from Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. The coinvariant space of <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{i,j}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{i,j}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis consisting of <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ss^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ss^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> together with <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1s},\ldots,E_{ns}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1s},\ldots,E_{ns}</annotation></semantics></math>. Similarly, the coinvariant space of <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Big(\bigoplus_{i}\mathbb{Q}\psi_{i}\Big)\oplus\Big(\bigoplus_{S}\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,S}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">⨁</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">⨁</mo><mi>S</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Big(\bigoplus_{i}\mathbb{Q}\psi_{i}\Big)\oplus\Big(\bigoplus_{S}\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,S}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis consisting of:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex63" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{s};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime}\}},\ 1\in S;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{s};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s,s^{\prime}\}},\ 1\in S;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p12.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p12.3.1" class="ltx_text">By examining the differential, one checks that <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{s,s^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{s,s^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used to eliminate <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{is}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{is}</annotation></semantics></math> can be used to eliminate <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,\{i,s\}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,\{i,s\}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the images of the remaining basis elements listed in (<a href="#S4.E19" title="In Proof of Proposition  for 
            
              
                =
                g
                2
              
            
          . ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) form a basis for <math id="S4.SS2.p12.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, as claimed.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, recall that <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+2})</annotation></semantics></math> is freely generated by boundary classes, which are permuted by the <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-action. One then sees that the coinvariant space <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis consisting of:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{1,S}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr};\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{1,S}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">Working with these bases, the same chain homotopy argument that works for <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math> gives a splitting of the map to <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">≀</mo><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+4})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}\wr\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. It remains to check that <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{2}_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{2}_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is exact at <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Just as for <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=3</annotation></semantics></math>, we do this by counting dimensions and computing the Euler characteristic. For simplicity, we assume <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>; the <math id="S4.SS2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math> case is similar.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">The dimension of <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{2,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2^{n}+2^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2^{n}+2^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Counting the bases (<a href="#S4.E19" title="In Proof of Proposition  for 
            
              
                =
                g
                2
              
            
          . ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>) and (<a href="#S4.E20" title="In Proof of Proposition  for 
            
              
                =
                g
                2
              
            
          . ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) then shows</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex64" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(V^{2}_{2,n})=(2^{n}+2^{n-1})-(3\cdot 2^{n}-n-1)+(2^{n}+2^{n-1}-n-1)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(V^{2}_{2,n})=(2^{n}+2^{n-1})-(3\cdot 2^{n}-n-1)+(2^{n}+2^{n-1}-n-1),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">which is 0, as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">We use the following lemma in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for <math id="S4.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and also in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 5.3. ‣ 5.2. A resolution of 
          
            
              ¯
              GK
            
            
              2
            
            
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The classes <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> have linearly independent image in <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.Thmthm4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> acts by transposing the last two marked points.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">These classes are <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-invariant, so it suffices to show that they are linearly independent in <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that there is a curve <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math> that intersects <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math> at finitely many points and is disjoint from <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> for all other subsets <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}\subset\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Fix <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+2</annotation></semantics></math> general points in <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> act so that the coordinates of the points in <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> are multiplied by <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> and the rest are multiplied by <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be the closure of this <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>-orbit. The image of <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{G}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔾</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{G}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in the open moduli space <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the remaining two points of <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are general in <math id="S4.SS2.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves the claim, and the lemma follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for <math id="S4.SS2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">We now consider the two-term complex <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V^{2}_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V^{2}_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex65" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n})\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p17.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p17.2.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{1,n})</annotation></semantics></math> is freely generated by the boundary classes <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the classes</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex66" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1,\ |S^{c}|\geq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2;\quad\quad\quad\delta_{0,S\cup\{s\}},\ |S|\geq 1,\ |S^{c}|\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p17.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p17.3.1" class="ltx_text">The differential is then given by <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr}\mapsto 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr}\mapsto 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S}\mapsto\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S}\mapsto\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math>. The statement of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> for <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> reduces to saying that the kernel of the differential is generated by <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr}</annotation></semantics></math>. This follows from Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, since the classes <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S},\ |S|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> have linearly independent images in <math id="S4.SS2.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This completes the proof of Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>The filtered map of filtered complexes</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now prove Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.5. The quotient 
          
            
              ¯
              GK ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, showing that the projection</p>
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a quasi-isomorphism for any <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\neq(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\neq(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. We do this by filtering both sides by the number of vertices and showing that the induced map between spectral sequences is an isomorphism at <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. From this it follows that (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In 4.3. The filtered map of filtered complexes ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) is a quasi-isomorphism by the spectral sequence comparison theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, Theorem 5.2.12]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.5. The quotient 
          
            
              ¯
              GK ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{p}\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}\subset\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>p</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{p}\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}\subset\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{p}\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}\subset\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>p</mi></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{p}\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}\subset\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the dg sub-vector spaces of <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, spanned by graphs with at least <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. So <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\bullet}\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\bullet}\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}^{\bullet}\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∙</mo></msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}^{\bullet}\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are decreasing filtrations of dg vector spaces, and (<a href="#S4.E21" title="In 4.3. The filtered map of filtered complexes ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a>) respects the filtrations. We now consider the spectral sequences associated to these filtrations and the induced map between them.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">On <math id="S4.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the associated graded dg vector spaces</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex67" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex67.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gr\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}\cong(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})\quad\quad\quad\mbox{ and }\quad\quad\quad\gr\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}\cong(\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n},0)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>gr</mi><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext> and </mtext><mi>gr</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mmultiscripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mmultiscripts><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n}\cong(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})\quad\quad\quad\mbox{ and }\quad\quad\quad\gr\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}\cong(\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n},0).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Say that the <em id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">loopless core</em> of a stable graph is the stable graph obtained by contracting all loop edges and increasing the genera of the adjacent vertices accordingly. Note that <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{loop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{loop}</annotation></semantics></math> does not change the loopless core, and hence <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes as a direct sum</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex68" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex68.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})\cong\bigoplus_{\Gamma}(\mathsf{GK}_{\Gamma},\delta_{loop})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⨁</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})\cong\bigoplus_{\Gamma}(\mathsf{GK}_{\Gamma},\delta_{loop}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> ranges over isomorphism classes of <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-marked stable graphs of genus <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> without loop edges and <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dg subspace spanned by graphs with loopless core <math id="S4.SS3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{loop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{loop}</annotation></semantics></math> acts independently on all vertices we furthermore have the isomorphism of dg vector spaces</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex69" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex69.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathsf{GK}_{\Gamma},\delta_{loop})\cong\left(\bigotimes_{v\in\mathrm{Vert}(\Gamma)}V^{k_{v}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes E(\Gamma)}\right)_{\Aut(\Gamma)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Vert</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathsf{GK}_{\Gamma},\delta_{loop})\cong\left(\bigotimes_{v\in\mathrm{Vert}(\Gamma)}V^{k_{v}}_{g_{v},n_{v}}\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes E(\Gamma)}\right)_{\Aut(\Gamma)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of structural edges of <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since taking coinvariants with respect to finite group actions commutes with taking cohomology we find that</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex70" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex70.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathsf{GK}_{\Gamma},\delta_{loop})\cong\left(\bigotimes_{v\in\mathrm{Vert}(\Gamma)}H(V^{k_{v}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes E(\Gamma)}\right)_{\Aut(\Gamma)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨂</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>Vert</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathsf{GK}_{\Gamma},\delta_{loop})\cong\left(\bigotimes_{v\in\mathrm{Vert}(\Gamma)}H(V^{k_{v}}_{g_{v},n_{v}})\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes E(\Gamma)}\right)_{\Aut(\Gamma)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{v}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{v}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for all vertices except one special vertex <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> which has <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{w}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{w}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In 4.1. The single vertex complex in weight zero ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), the right hand side vanishes if <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has a non-special vertex of positive genus. Otherwise, the right hand side is</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex71" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex71.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(H(V^{2}_{g_{w},n_{w}})\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes E(\Gamma)}\right)_{\Aut(\Gamma)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>Aut</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(H(V^{2}_{g_{w},n_{w}})\otimes\mathbb{Q}[-1]^{\otimes E(\Gamma)}\right)_{\Aut(\Gamma)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">In other words, <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop})</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with a graded vector space of stable graphs without loop edges where all vertices have genus 0 except possibly one special vertex <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> that is decorated by <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(V^{2}_{g_{w},n_{w}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>V</mi><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(V^{2}_{g_{w},n_{w}})</annotation></semantics></math>, oriented by a total ordering of the structural edges.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The differential on the <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page of our spectral sequence is then induced by the part <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> of the original differential. Our goal is to show that the projection</span></p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(H(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop}),\delta_{split})\to(\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{split})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mmultiscripts><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow></mrow><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mmultiscripts><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H(\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{loop}),\delta_{split})\to(\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n},\delta_{split}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">is a quasi-isomorphism. The right-hand side is also a graded vector space of stable graphs. Now all vertices have genus 0, but loop edges are allowed, and one special vertex <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> is decorated by <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n_{w}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>w</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n_{w}})</annotation></semantics></math>, modulo the image of <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{loop}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{loop}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying Proposition <a href="#S4.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, we see that (<a href="#S4.E22" title="In Proof of Theorem . ‣ 4.3. The filtered map of filtered complexes ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>) is surjective, with kernel <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by stable graphs in which all vertices have genus 0 except one special vertex of genus 1 that is decorated with <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{irr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{irr}</annotation></semantics></math>. Equivalently, <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative graph complex generated by graphs with one special vertex in which all vertices have valence at least 3 except the special vertex, which may also have valence 1 or 2. We prove the acyclicity of <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1}\subset K_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1}\subset K_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the dg subspace spanned by graphs in which the special vertex has valence <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\geq 2}\subset K_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\geq 2}\subset K_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> the complementary subspace spanned by graphs having a special vertex of valence <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The differential <math id="S4.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes accordingly into the following pieces</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex72" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
