\lxSVG@closescope {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">In both parts, there is precisely one new structural edge, and the ordering is chosen so that the new edge comes first and the relative ordering of the old edges is preserved. It is straightforward to verify that applying <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}</annotation></semantics></math> to generators for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> produces a sum of generators for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> squares to zero (as do <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In pictures, we draw graphs in <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with filled black vertices, to distinguish them from graphs in <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The graph complex <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is closely related to the graph complexes studied by Fresse, Turchin, and the second author in the context of the embedding calculus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
For suitable submanifolds <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\subset{\mathbb{R}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> they showed that the rational homotopy groups of the (long) embedding spaces modulo immersions <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathrm{Emb}}_{\partial}(M,{\mathbb{R}}^{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Emb</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∂</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathrm{Emb}}_{\partial}(M,{\mathbb{R}}^{N})</annotation></semantics></math> can be expressed as the cohomology of a graph complex <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{HGC}_{\bar{A},N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖧𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{HGC}_{\bar{A},N}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which external vertices are labeled by elements of the augmentation ideal <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{A}</annotation></semantics></math> of a dg commutative algebra model of the one-point-compactification <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M\cup\{\infty\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M\cup\{\infty\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, the cohomology groups of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathrm{Emb}}_{\partial}(M,{\mathbb{R}}^{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Emb</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>∂</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathrm{Emb}}_{\partial}(M,{\mathbb{R}}^{N})</annotation></semantics></math> are computed by the Chevalley complex of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{HGC}_{\bar{A},N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖧𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{HGC}_{\bar{A},N}</annotation></semantics></math>, and this latter complex can be identified with a complex of (possibly) disconnected graphs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, if <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> is a union of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> copies of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{R}}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{R}}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{2\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{2\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the corresponding model <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{A}</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis consisting of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> in degree zero, <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\omega_{1},\ldots,\omega_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\omega_{1},\ldots,\omega_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in degree <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, with product given by <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon^{2}=\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon^{2}=\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\omega=\omega," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\omega=\omega,</annotation></semantics></math> and
<math id="S5.Thmthm1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\omega_{j}=\omega^{2}=\omega_{i}\omega=\omega_{i}\omega_{j}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ω</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ω</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\omega_{j}=\omega^{2}=\omega_{i}\omega=\omega_{i}\omega_{j}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Then, up to unimportant degree shifts, <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> may be identified with a subquotient of the Chevalley complex of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{HGC}_{\bar{A},N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖧𝖦𝖢</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{HGC}_{\bar{A},N}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> even, corresponding to genus <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> graphs without loop edges that contain each decoration <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> exactly once, and that are connected after fusing the <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>- and <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated legs.
We shall not need this connection to the embedding calculus elsewhere in the paper, and leave the more precise comparison of the cohomology of <math id="S5.Thmthm1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and the cohomology of emedding spaces to future work.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>A resolution of <math id="S5.SS2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to relate <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by a zigzag of quasi-isomorphisms. To this end, we now construct a quasi-isomorphism <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}\xrightarrow{\sim}\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}\xrightarrow{\sim}\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> also maps naturally onto <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Roughly speaking <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is constructed from <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by resolving the space of decorations <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})</annotation></semantics></math>, as in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>.
One can also construct a quasi-isomorphism <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>; see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. The construction of the map is explicit, but the proof that it is a quasi-isomorphism uses the resolution <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}\xrightarrow{\sim}\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}\xrightarrow{\sim}\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so we have chosen to focus on the zig-zag.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Concretely, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph complex analogous to <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> generated by graphs with the following features. All vertices have genus 0 and there is exactly one special vertex with extra decoration. There are no loop edges at non-special vertices. The special vertex is decorated with one of the following:</p>
<ul id="S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A symbol <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of the <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> half-edges at the special vertex such that <math id="S5.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq|S|\leq k-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq|S|\leq k-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A symbol <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is a half-edge at the special vertex.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A symbol <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S5.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math> are distinct half-edges at the special vertex.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We impose the relations <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}=\delta_{0,A^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}=E_{ji}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}=E_{ji}</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, if there is a loop at the special vertex with half-edges <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we impose the <em id="S5.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">loop relations</em>:</p>
<table id="S5.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(23)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S5.E23.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:314.0pt;height:13.8pt;vertical-align:-7.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-42.5pt,1.9pt) scale(0.787017761031166,0.787017761031166) ;">
<p id="S5.E23.2.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.E23.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}=0;\quad\quad\psi_{t}=\psi_{t^{\prime}}=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{{t\in A,t^{\prime}\notin A}}\delta_{0,A};\quad\quad E_{it}=E_{it^{\prime}}=0;\quad\quad\delta_{0,A}=0\mbox{ if }t,t^{\prime}\in S\mbox{ (or $t,t^{\prime}\in S^{c}$)}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∉</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext> (or </mtext><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mtext>)</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}=0;\quad\quad\psi_{t}=\psi_{t^{\prime}}=\frac{1}{2}\sum_{{t\in A,t^{\prime}\notin A}}\delta_{0,A};\quad\quad E_{it}=E_{it^{\prime}}=0;\quad\quad\delta_{0,A}=0\mbox{ if }t,t^{\prime}\in S\mbox{ (or $t,t^{\prime}\in S^{c}$)}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">As usual, each generator comes equipped with an ordering of the structural edges, and permuting this ordering induces multiplication by the sign of the permutation. Formally, these decorations should be interpreted as elements of a dg vector space resolving <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})</annotation></semantics></math>, as in Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. In this resolution, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> have degree 2, while <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> has degree <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, a generator with <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> structural edges has degree <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+2</annotation></semantics></math> unless its decoration is <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case the degree is <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We continue to depict the decoration at the special vertex by adding combinatorial features to the graph. The depictions of <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly as in section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2. Graphical depiction of boundary and 
          
            ψ
          
        -class decorations ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>.
We depict the decoration <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> distinct half-edges incident to the special vertex, by marking the two half-edges with arrows as follows.</p>
<table id="S5.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex76"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.Ex76.m1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_markedasmath" height="50.97" overflow="visible" style="vertical-align:-16.09px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 44.65 50.97" width="44.65"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,50.97) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(22.33,0) translate(0,19.96)"><g stroke-width="1.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 1.96 0 C 1.96 1.08 1.08 1.96 0 1.96 C -1.08 1.96 -1.96 1.08 -1.96 0 C -1.96 -1.08 -1.08 -1.96 0 -1.96 C 1.08 -1.96 1.96 -1.08 1.96 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#FFFFFF;" stroke="#FFFFFF" stroke-width="0.6pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 1.96 0 C 1.96 1.08 1.08 1.96 0 1.96 C -1.08 1.96 -1.96 1.08 -1.96 0 C -1.96 -1.08 -1.08 -1.96 0 -1.96 C 1.08 -1.96 1.96 -1.08 1.96 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -10.38 16.94)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.5em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.29em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.41" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="20.76"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S5.Ex76.m1.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -1.7 2.03 L -13.61 16.22"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 1.7 2.03 L 13.61 16.22"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 2.29 -1.32 L 17.05 -9.84"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 -2.65 L 0 -19.69"></path><path style="fill:none" d="M -2.29 -1.32 L -17.05 -9.84"></path></g></g></svg></span></td>
