<div id="S5.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.2</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The differential on <math id="S5.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-defined and squares to zero.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">First, we need to show that this differential is well-defined, i.e., that it respects the loop relations (<a href="#S5.E24" title="In 5.2. A resolution of 
          
            
              ¯
              GK
            
            
              2
            
            
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>).
That it respects the first and third relations is relatively straightforward. To see that it respects the second loop relation,
note that the differential applied to the left-hand side is</span></p>
