</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">We need to compare this to <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> applied to the right-hand side of (<a href="#S5.E25" title="In 5.2. A resolution of 
          
            
              ¯
              GK
            
            
              2
            
            
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>). The fourth and seventh terms together match <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> applied to the second term of (<a href="#S5.E25" title="In 5.2. A resolution of 
          
            
              ¯
              GK
            
            
              2
            
            
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>), with opposite sign. Likewise, the second and the eighth term of (<a href="#S5.E29" title="In Proof. ‣ 5.2. A resolution of 
          
            
              ¯
              GK
            
            
              2
            
            
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a>) match <math id="S5.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> applied to the first term of (<a href="#S5.E25" title="In 5.2. A resolution of 
          
            
              ¯
              GK
            
            
              2
            
            
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">25</span></a>). The cancellation of the remaining terms is similar.
∎</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.3</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There is a quasi-isomorphism</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex80" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex80.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">that sets <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> to zero and imposes the relation <math id="S5.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}+\psi_{j}-\displaystyle\sum_{i\in S,j\in S^{c}}\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}+\psi_{j}-\displaystyle\sum_{i\in S,j\in S^{c}}\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">We first verify that this projection is a well-defined map of complexes. It clearly respects the differential on all generators where the special vertex is decorated by <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider a generator <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma\in\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> whose special vertex is decorated by <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> also has an <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated special vertex and is hence sent to zero.
Furthermore <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{res}\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{res}\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is sent to zero since its special vertex is just decorated by the relation between <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>- and <math id="S5.SS2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>-classes.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">To see that this projection is a quasi-isomorphism, we filter both sides by the number of vertices and consider the associated spectral sequences.
We claim that the map <math id="S5.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a quasi-isomorphism already on the associated graded complex.
Proceeding as in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. The filtered map of filtered complexes ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> one can see it therefore suffices to consider the complex associated to the special vertex.
The differential on the associated graded of <math id="S5.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero, and the differential on the associated graded of <math id="S5.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> does not add any edges. We can therefore consider separate cases according to whether or not there is a loop at the special vertex.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">If there is no loop, then the loop relations listed above do not come into play, and the statement boils down to Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>, which says that</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex81" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex81.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus_{1\leq i&lt;j\leq k}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}\to\Big(\bigoplus_{1\leq i\leq k}\mathbb{Q}\psi_{i}\Big)\oplus\bigoplus\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,S}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">⊕</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{1\leq i&lt;j\leq k}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}\to\Big(\bigoplus_{1\leq i\leq k}\mathbb{Q}\psi_{i}\Big)\oplus\bigoplus\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">is a resolution of <math id="S5.SS2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to consider the case where there is a loop at the special vertex. Let <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t,t^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t,t^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the last two marked points on <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k+2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We must show that the complex</span></p>
<table id="S5.E30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(30)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E30.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\bigoplus_{1\leq i&lt;j\leq k}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}\to\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\}}\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,A\cup\{t\}},\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}/\sim" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">→</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mi>ℚ</mi><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">∼</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus_{1\leq i&lt;j\leq k}\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}\to\bigoplus_{A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\}}\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,A\cup\{t\}},\to H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}/\sim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p15.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p15.2.1" class="ltx_text">is exact, where <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math> is the quotient by the loop relations <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, we begin by taking the <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-coinvariants of the resolution of <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k+2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k+2})</annotation></semantics></math> from Proposition <a href="#S3.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.6. Resolutions of 
          
            
              ⁢
              
                
                H
                2
              
              
                
                
                  (
                  
                    
                    
                      ¯
                      M
                    
                    
                      
                        
                        
                        
                      
                      
                        0
                        ,
                        n
                      
                    
                  
                  ) ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a>. Then the <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-coinvariant space of the first term <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigoplus\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigoplus\mathbb{Q}E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis consisting of</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex82" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex82.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij},\ 1\leq i&lt;j\leq k;\quad\quad\quad E_{it},\ 1\leq i\leq k;\quad\quad\quad E_{tt^{\prime}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="3.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij},\ 1\leq i&lt;j\leq k;\quad\quad\quad E_{it},\ 1\leq i\leq k;\quad\quad\quad E_{tt^{\prime}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p15.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p15.3.1" class="ltx_text">Similarly, the <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-coinvariant space of the second term <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\bigoplus\mathbb{Q}\psi_{i})\oplus(\bigoplus\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,A})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\bigoplus\mathbb{Q}\psi_{i})\oplus(\bigoplus\mathbb{Q}\delta_{0,A})</annotation></semantics></math> has a basis consisting of</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex83" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S5.Ex83.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:327.8pt;height:10.3pt;vertical-align:-4.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-58.4pt,1.8pt) scale(0.737169063613423,0.737169063613423) ;">
<p id="S5.Ex83.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.Ex83.m1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i},\ 1\leq i\leq k;\quad\quad\psi_{t};\quad\quad\delta_{0,A},\ A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\},\ |S|\geq 2;\quad\quad\delta_{0,A\cup\{t\}},\ A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\},\ |A|\geq 1,\ |A^{c}|\geq 1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i},\ 1\leq i\leq k;\quad\quad\psi_{t};\quad\quad\delta_{0,A},\ A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\},\ |S|\geq 2;\quad\quad\delta_{0,A\cup\{t\}},\ A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\},\ |A|\geq 1,\ |A^{c}|\geq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p15.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p15.4.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmthm4" title="Lemma 4.4. ‣ 4.2. The single vertex complex in weight 2 ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a>, the subspace spanned by the basis elements <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ A\subset\{1,\ldots,k\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|A|\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|A|\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> maps isomorphically onto its image in <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,k+2})_{\mathbb{S}_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is precisely the span of the loop relations. We can then use <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{it}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{it}</annotation></semantics></math> to eliminate <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{tt^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{tt^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> to eliminate <math id="S5.SS2.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and see that (<a href="#S5.E30" title="In Proof. ‣ 5.2. A resolution of 
          
            
              ¯
              GK
            
            
              2
            
            
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">30</span></a>) is exact, as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Completing the zig-zag</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now complete the zig-zag from <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by producing a quasi-isomorphism</p>
<table id="S5.Ex84" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex84.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Phi:\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}\xrightarrow{\sim}X_{g,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">∼</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi:\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}\xrightarrow{\sim}X_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph in <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> structural edges. Say that the half-edges incident to external vertices are <em id="S5.SS3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">legs</em>.</p>
<ul id="S5.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the special vertex of <math id="S5.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is decorated by <math id="S5.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,A}</annotation></semantics></math> we define <math id="S5.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\Gamma)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\Gamma)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the special vertex of <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is decorated by <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, then we let <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> be the marked graph obtained by deleting the special vertex, making the half-edges incident to the special vertex into legs, and decorating the external vertices on <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> and the other newly created external vertices by <math id="S5.I5.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
