<p id="S5.I5.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note in particular that if the half-edge opposite <math id="S5.I5.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is a leg, say incident to an external vertex with some marking <math id="S5.I5.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.I5.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> will have a connected component that is an <math id="S5.I5.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\omega,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\omega,j)</annotation></semantics></math> edge:</p>
