<p id="S5.SS3.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">Note that in all terms there is at least one <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-leg.
Next we look at <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}^{s}\Phi(\Gamma)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}^{s}\Phi(\Gamma)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> removes the special vertex. Applying <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}</annotation></semantics></math> to the right-hand side of (<a href="#S5.E31" title="In 2nd item ‣ 5.3. Completing the zig-zag ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">31</span></a>) produces several graphs, by fusing a subset of <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-legs together to produce a new <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-leg, or fusing a subset of the <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-legs to one <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-leg.
The two cases in which all the <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-hairs are fused to one <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-leg precisely contribute (<a href="#S5.E34" title="In Proof. ‣ 5.3. Completing the zig-zag ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">34</span></a>). The cases where one or more <math id="S5.SS3.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-legs remain contribute the terms (<a href="#S5.E35" title="In Proof. ‣ 5.3. Completing the zig-zag ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>).
To confirm the signs, note that in all cases considered the newly added edge is the first in the ordering.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">Hence we conclude that
<math id="S5.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}\Phi(\Gamma)=\Phi(\delta_{split}^{s}\Gamma+\delta_{res}\Gamma)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}\Phi(\Gamma)=\Phi(\delta_{split}^{s}\Gamma+\delta_{res}\Gamma),</annotation></semantics></math>
as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 5.5</span></span><span id="S5.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The map <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is a surjective quasi-isomorphism with kernel generated by graphs in which the special vertex is decorated by some <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math>, and by symmetric combinations of graphs with <math id="S5.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations on either half of some edge:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex93" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

