<div id="S5.SS3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We begin by showing that <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. Let <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph in <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will construct a graph <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\Gamma}\in\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Gamma}\in\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\widetilde{\Gamma})=\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\widetilde{\Gamma})=\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
First, consider the case where <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has at least one <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated external vertex. Then we build <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math> by joining the <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>- and <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated external vertices to a new internal vertex, decorated by <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the legs at the two <math id="S5.SS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> decorations:</span></p>
