</table>
<p id="S5.SS3.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">In either case, <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\widetilde{\Gamma})=\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\widetilde{\Gamma})=\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here we also use that at least one of the <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-legs is adjacent to an internal vertex. Indeed, if this were not the case, then the graph would be either a single <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\omega,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\omega,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge, or the union of an <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\omega,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\omega,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge and an <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\omega,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\omega,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge. In either case, we would have <math id="S5.SS3.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n&lt;3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n&lt;3</annotation></semantics></math>, which we have excluded from consideration.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">The map takes graphs with decoration <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math> to zero, and the orientation data ensures that symmetric combinations of <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations on paired half-edges also map to zero. Otherwise, distinct generators for <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> map to distinct generators of <math id="S5.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so nothing else is in the kernel.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">It remains to check that <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J:=\ker\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ker</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J:=\ker\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic. Decompose <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J=J_{\delta}\oplus J_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J=J_{\delta}\oplus J_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math> are linear combinations of graphs with <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>- or <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations, respectively.
The differential <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> then splits into the following pieces</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex96" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

