<p id="S5.SS3.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">In these graphical depictions, <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> replaces a marked edge with a symmetric combination of <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations on its paired half-edges. This map gives a bijection between bases for <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{\psi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>ψ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{\psi}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is hence an isomorphism.
It then follows by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.2. Quasi-isomorphisms and acyclicity ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> that <math id="S5.SS3.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic, as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Propositions <a href="#S5.Thmthm3" title="Proposition 5.3. ‣ 5.2. A resolution of 
          
            
              ¯
              GK
            
            
              2
            
            
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  g
                  ,
                  n ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a> and <a href="#S5.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 5.5. ‣ 5.3. Completing the zig-zag ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a> gives the desired zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms
<math id="S5.SS3.p11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}\leftarrow\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}\xrightarrow{\Phi}X_{g,n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">Φ</mo></mover><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}\leftarrow\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}\xrightarrow{\Phi}X_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although we do not fully understand the cohomology of <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can describe many nontrivial classes.
We do so by identifying a quasi-isomorphic subcomplex <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and constructing an involution on <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> that simplifies the differential. In this simplified subcomplex, we find direct summands whose cohomology we can either compute or bound from below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that every connected component of a generator <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> contains an external vertex labeled by <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A connected component that contains an <math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decoration is an <em id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright"><math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-component</em>. All other connected components are <em id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright"><math id="S6.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-components</em>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}\subset X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}\subset X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subspace spanned by generators in which the union of all <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-components contains no internal vertices and at most one decoration from <math id="S6.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\dots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\dots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, if <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a generator for <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the union of its <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-components is either empty or consists of an <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge, an <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge, or one of each. Here are two examples:</p>
<table id="S6.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

