\lxSVG@closescope }}}
\lxSVG@closescope {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}}\ .</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.3</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The graded subspace <math id="S6.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}\subset X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}\subset X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a subcomplex.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a generator for <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The differential is a sum of two parts <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}</annotation></semantics></math>. The part <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> acts separately on the internal vertices of each connected component. It cannot create internal vertices in a component that does not already have any, and it does change the decorations. So <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is a linear combination of generators for <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now consider the terms that appear in <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, which are obtained by joining external vertices of one or several components. If at least one of the joined components is an <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-component, then the resulting graph is a generator for <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. However, if all components that are joined are <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-components, then the resulting graph has either a loop or two <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-labeled external vertices adjacent to the same internal vertex, and hence is zero in <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.4</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The inclusion <math id="S6.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}\subset X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}\subset X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Filter the complexes <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by the number of internal vertices in <math id="S6.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-components.
We claim that the inclusion induces a quasi-isomorphism between the associated graded complexes with respect to this filtration.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">The associated graded complexes have differential <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}^{\epsilon,\epsilon}+\delta_{split}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}^{\epsilon,\epsilon}+\delta_{split}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}^{\epsilon,\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}^{\epsilon,\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> the part of <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> (as defined in the proof of Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm3" title="Lemma 6.3. ‣ 6.1. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ 6. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>) that joins <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-legs of <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-components only and <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}^{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}^{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> the part of <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> that splits vertices in <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-components only. Note that the differential on <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is 0.
Furthermore, we have the direct sum decomposition</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex99" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex99.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{g,n},\delta_{join}^{\epsilon,\epsilon}+\delta_{split}^{\epsilon})=X^{\star}_{g,n}\oplus(W,\delta_{join}^{\epsilon,\epsilon}+\delta_{split}^{\epsilon})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{g,n},\delta_{join}^{\epsilon,\epsilon}+\delta_{split}^{\epsilon})=X^{\star}_{g,n}\oplus(W,\delta_{join}^{\epsilon,\epsilon}+\delta_{split}^{\epsilon}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">with <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W\subset X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W\subset X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> spanned by graphs that are not generators for <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Generators of <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> are graphs in which the <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-components contain an internal vertex or more than one leg labelled <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1,\dots,n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1,\dots,n</annotation></semantics></math>.
We further decompose</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex100" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex100.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=W^{\prime}\oplus W^{\prime\prime}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊕</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=W^{\prime}\oplus W^{\prime\prime},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by graphs that do not contain an <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge, and <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is spanned by those that do.
Note that <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic (up to degree shift) to the <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> summand that arises in <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g-1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g-1,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, so it suffices to show that <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.
To this end consider the decomposition of graded vector spaces</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex101" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex101.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}=\hbox to106.19pt{\vbox to35.1pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 53.09525pt\lower-8.81778pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} {}{}{}{{}}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\hbox{\hbox{{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} {{}{}{{{}}{{}}{{}}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{{}{}{{
{}{}}}{
{}{}}
{{}{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}}{{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}{}{{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}
{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
}{{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}}\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} \lxSVG@transformcm{1.0}{0.0}{0.0}{1.0}{-53.09525pt}{-2.35977pt}\lxSVG@begingroup@{transform} \pgfsys@hbox{58}\lxSVG@closescope }}}{{{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces{}}}{{}}{{}}{{}}}}
\lxSVG@closescope }}}
{
