\lxSVG@closescope {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p6.6.1" class="ltx_text">This is map is injective; it has a one-sided inverse obtained by removing both new vertices and the edge between them, and adding an external vertex labeled <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> to each of the dangling edges.
This map is also surjective, since the unique <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decoration in an <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-component of a graph in <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> must be adjacent to an internal vertex.
(If it was adjacent to an <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated leg the graph would not be in <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\omega)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\omega)</annotation></semantics></math>-edges are forbidden in <math id="S6.SS1.p6.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>.)
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We note that in the above proof the presence of the part of the differential <math id="S6.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}^{\omega}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ω</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}^{\omega}</annotation></semantics></math> played no role. The same proof also shows the following auxiliary result, which we will use in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 7.2. ‣ 7. The weight 2 compactly supported cohomology of 
        
          
            
            M
            g ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 6.5</span></span><span id="S6.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The inclusion <math id="S6.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{\star}_{g},\delta_{join}^{\epsilon}+\delta_{split})\to(X_{g},\delta_{join}^{\epsilon}+\delta_{split})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{\star}_{g},\delta_{join}^{\epsilon}+\delta_{split})\to(X_{g},\delta_{join}^{\epsilon}+\delta_{split})</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism of dg vector spaces.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>A simplifying involution</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now construct an involution of <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> that simplifies the differential. This involution is given by reattaching subsets of the <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated external vertices in all possible ways and motivates the introduction of the subcomplex <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>; see Remark <a href="#S6.Thmthm6" title="Remark 6.6. ‣ 6.2. A simplifying involution ‣ 6. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a generator for <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{S}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{S}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> for the sum of all graphs obtained by reattaching a subset <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decorated external vertices of <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to internal vertices, in all possible ways without forming loop edges:</p>
