<p id="S7.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">The vertical map is a quasi-isomorphism by Lemma <a href="#S6.Thmthm5" title="Lemma 6.5. ‣ 6.1. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ 6. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.5</span></a>.
It hence suffices to show that the diagonal inclusion</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex123" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex123.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H_{g},\delta_{split})\to(X_{g},\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}^{\epsilon})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H_{g},\delta_{split})\to(X_{g},\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}^{\epsilon})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">is a quasi-isomorphism.
Each complex splits, as in the proof of Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 6.4. ‣ 6.1. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ 6. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>,
into one piece generated by graphs that do not have any <math id="S7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge and a complementary piece spanned by those that do. So, we write <math id="S7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for the subcomplexes of <math id="S7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, spanned by graphs that do not have any <math id="S7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge. Similarly, we write <math id="S7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime\prime}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime\prime}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\prime\prime}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\prime\prime}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> for the subcomplexes spanned by graphs with an <math id="S7.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge. We then have</span></p>
<table id="S7.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S7.Ex124"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex124.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex124.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cong H_{g}^{\prime}\oplus H_{g}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cong H_{g}^{\prime}\oplus H_{g}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex124.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle X_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle X_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S7.Ex124.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\cong X_{g}^{\prime}\oplus X_{g}^{\prime\prime}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊕</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\cong X_{g}^{\prime}\oplus X_{g}^{\prime\prime}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p5.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p5.4.1" class="ltx_text">Note that <math id="S7.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}^{\prime\prime}\cong H_{g-1}^{\prime}[-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}^{\prime\prime}\cong H_{g-1}^{\prime}[-1]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g}^{\prime\prime}\cong X_{g-1}^{\prime}[-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g}^{\prime\prime}\cong X_{g-1}^{\prime}[-1]</annotation></semantics></math>, so
it suffices to show that <math id="S7.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}^{\prime}\subset X^{\prime}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}^{\prime}\subset X^{\prime}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We furthermore have a direct sum decomposition of complexes</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex125" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex125.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{g}^{\prime},\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}^{\epsilon})\cong(H_{g}^{\prime},\delta_{split})\oplus(U,\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}^{\epsilon})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{g}^{\prime},\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}^{\epsilon})\cong(H_{g}^{\prime},\delta_{split})\oplus(U,\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}^{\epsilon}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\subset X_{g}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\subset X_{g}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the subcomplex spanned by graphs with at least one <math id="S7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-leg.
It remains to show that <math id="S7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We proceed as in Proposition <a href="#S6.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 6.4. ‣ 6.1. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ 6. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a> and decompose</span></p>
