<p id="S7.p9.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p9.8.1" class="ltx_text">with <math id="S7.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{1}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{1}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S7.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{2}^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{2}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>) being spanned by graphs for which the vertex <math id="S7.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> has valency <math id="S7.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> (resp. <math id="S7.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>).
Now the piece of the differential</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex136" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex136.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime\prime}:U_{2}^{\prime\prime}\to U_{1}^{\prime\prime}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime\prime}:U_{2}^{\prime\prime}\to U_{1}^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p9.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p9.9.1" class="ltx_text">is a bijection, since there are only two <math id="S7.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-legs.
Hence by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.2. Quasi-isomorphisms and acyclicity ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> we have that <math id="S7.p9.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\coker(D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\coker(D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is acyclic, and hence so are <math id="S7.p9.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\coker(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>coker</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\coker(D)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.p9.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>. This proves the proposition.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S7.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a>.</h6>
<div id="S7.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Propositions <a href="#S6.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 6.4. ‣ 6.1. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ 6. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.4</span></a>, <a href="#S6.Thmthm8" title="Proposition 6.8. ‣ 6.2. A simplifying involution ‣ 6. A quasi-isomorphic subcomplex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.8</span></a>, and <a href="#S7.Thmthm2" title="Proposition 7.2. ‣ 7. The weight 2 compactly supported cohomology of 
        
          
            
            M
            g ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>, the composition</span></p>
<table id="S7.Ex137" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S7.Ex137.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}\hookrightarrow(X^{\star}_{g},\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}^{\epsilon})\xrightarrow{\Phi}(X^{\star}_{g},\delta_{split}+\delta_{join})\hookrightarrow X_{g}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">Φ</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">↪</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}\hookrightarrow(X^{\star}_{g},\delta_{split}+\delta_{join}^{\epsilon})\xrightarrow{\Phi}(X^{\star}_{g},\delta_{split}+\delta_{join})\hookrightarrow X_{g}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S7.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">is a quasi-isomorphism. The cohomology of the right-hand side is <math id="S7.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr^{2}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr^{2}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})</annotation></semantics></math>. The cohomology of <math id="S7.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is expressed via Lemma <a href="#S7.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 7.1. ‣ 7. The weight 2 compactly supported cohomology of 
        
          
            
            M
            g ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a> through the cohomology of the complexes <math id="S7.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g^{\prime},n^{\prime})}</annotation></semantics></math> that compute <math id="S7.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr^{0}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>gr</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr^{0}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g^{\prime},n^{\prime}})</annotation></semantics></math> by Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 4.1 ([]). ‣ 4.1. The single vertex complex in weight zero ‣ 4. Filtration by the number of vertices ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, and Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Direct summands with marked points</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a generator for <math id="S8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let us now assume <math id="S8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that each such generator has some number of <math id="S8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations and precisely two <math id="S8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations. The remaining external vertices are decorated by a bijection to <math id="S8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each component has at least one decoration from <math id="S8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\epsilon,\omega\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\epsilon,\omega\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8.1</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">A connected component of <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">isolated</em> if it contains no decorations from <math id="S8.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n,\epsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n,\epsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In other words, a component is isolated if all of its external vertices are decorated with <math id="S8.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Components that do have a decoration from <math id="S8.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n,\epsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n,\epsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="S8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">non-isolated</em>. We decompose <math id="S8.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> according to the number of <math id="S8.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> decorations that are contained non-isolated components.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 8.2</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the graded subspaces of <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> spanned by graphs with <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> of their <math id="S8.Thmthm2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations contained in non-isolated components, respectively.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">No term of the differential <math id="S8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\delta}</annotation></semantics></math> can make an isolated connected component into a non-isolated one or vice versa, so
we have a direct sum decomposition of dg vector spaces</p>
<table id="S8.E37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(37)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{\star}_{g,n},\tilde{\delta})=H_{g,n}\oplus J_{g,n}\oplus K_{g,n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{\star}_{g,n},\tilde{\delta})=H_{g,n}\oplus J_{g,n}\oplus K_{g,n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We will study the cohomology of the summands separately. Unfortunately, we have nothing to say about <math id="S8.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(K_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(K_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.1. </span>The cohomology of <math id="S8.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The summand <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> contributes to the cohomology of <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> only when <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and that contribution is well-understood.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8.3</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have <math id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g,1}\cong H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g,1}\cong H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g,n}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g,n}=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S8.Thmthm3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the definition of <math id="S8.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the non-isolated components that do not contain <math id="S8.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations consist of either an <math id="S8.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge, an <math id="S8.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge, or one of each. If <math id="S8.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then none of the generators for <math id="S8.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> are in <math id="S8.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the generators for <math id="S8.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> are precisely the graphs of the form</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex138" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex138.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hbox to63.74pt{\vbox to19.08pt{\pgfpicture\makeatletter\hbox{\hskip 37.9926pt\lower-9.53984pt\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} \lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke} \lxSVG@begingroup@{fill} \lxSVG@setlinewidth{\the\pgflinewidth}\lxSVG@begingroup@{stroke-width} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\nullfont\hbox to0.0pt{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} {{}}\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces\hbox{\hbox{{\lxSVG@begingroup@{_scopebegin} {{}{{{}}}{{}}{}{}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{}{}{}{}{}{{}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M -26.45 0 C -26.45 7.14 -32.23 12.92 -39.37 12.92 C -46.51 12.92 -52.29 7.14 -52.29 0 C -52.29 -7.14 -46.51 -12.92 -39.37 -12.92 C -32.23 -12.92 -26.45 -7.14 -26.45 0 Z M -39.37 0}{fill:none} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
