<p id="S8.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The differential acts by <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math> on the isolated component, so <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposes as a direct sum of subcomplexes determined by the genus of the isolated component. The subcomplex where the non-isolated part has genus <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as a tensor product <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\star}_{h,n}\otimes G^{(h^{\prime},1)}[-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\star}_{h,n}\otimes G^{(h^{\prime},1)}[-1]</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h+h^{\prime}=g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h+h^{\prime}=g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\star}_{h,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\star}_{h,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph complex perfectly analogous to <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\star}_{h,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\star}_{h,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, except that each generator has exactly one <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> decoration instead of two, and this <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decoration must be part of a non-isolated component.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            The latter condition is automatically satisfied if <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We then have a decomposition</p>
<table id="S8.E38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(38)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{g,n}=\bigoplus_{h+h^{\prime}=g}J^{\star}_{h,n}\otimes G^{(h^{\prime},1)}[-1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{g,n}=\bigoplus_{h+h^{\prime}=g}J^{\star}_{h,n}\otimes G^{(h^{\prime},1)}[-1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The degree shift on the factor <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(h^{\prime},1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(h^{\prime},1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is due to the additional structural edge that contains the <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> decoration.
The degree of a generator for <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\star}_{h,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\star}_{h,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of structural edges plus one, just as for <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g^{\prime},n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g^{\prime},n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The cohomology of <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\star}_{h,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\star}_{h,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is difficult to evaluate when <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is large; we leave its study for <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> to future work. Theorems <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a> use only the cohomology of <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\star}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\star}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\star}_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\star}_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which we now describe.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.3. </span>The cohomology of <math id="S8.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\star}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\star}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S8.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now explain how the cohomology of <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\star}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\star}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is closely related to <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1})</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}\subset J^{*}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}\subset J^{*}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subcomplex generated by graphs with no <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> decorations. Each generator for <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a rooted tree with <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> leaves labeled <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a root labeled <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the complementary graded subspace generated by graphs with an <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decoration. Since the genus is zero, there are no <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edges, and no connected component can have both an <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decoration and also an <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decoration. So each generator for <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has two connected components, an <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge and an <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-rooted tree with <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> leaves labeled <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,\ldots,\hat{\jmath},\ldots n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ȷ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,\ldots,\hat{\jmath},\ldots n\}</annotation></semantics></math>. As an immediate consequence, we see that</p>
