<p id="S8.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the internal arrows on <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}\cong G^{(0,n+1)}[-2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}\cong G^{(0,n+1)}[-2]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(V_{0},\delta_{split})\cong\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}G^{(0,n)}[-2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(V_{0},\delta_{split})\cong\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}G^{(0,n)}[-2]</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(V_{1})\cong W_{0}H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1})[-2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(V_{1})\cong W_{0}H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1})[-2]</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)!</annotation></semantics></math>, supported in degree <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(V_{0},\delta_{split})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(V_{0},\delta_{split})</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n(n-2)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n(n-2)!</annotation></semantics></math>, supported in degree <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.2. Quasi-isomorphisms and acyclicity ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>(<a href="#S2.I1.i1" title="item 1 ‣ Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2.2. Quasi-isomorphisms and acyclicity ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), the first statement of Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 8.4. ‣ 8.3. The cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                
                J
                ⋆
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  0
                  ,
                  n ‣ 8. Direct summands with marked points ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a> follows if we can show that</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex143" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex143.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}\colon H(V_{0},\delta_{split})\to H(V_{1})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}\colon H(V_{0},\delta_{split})\to H(V_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS3.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">is surjective. For <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{j}\subset{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{j}\subset{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the locally closed divisor parametrizing 1-nodal curves where <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math> collide. Then <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{j}\cong{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{j}\cong{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the union <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1}\sqcup\Big(\bigsqcup_{j}D_{j}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>⊔</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">⨆</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1}\sqcup\Big(\bigsqcup_{j}D_{j}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math> is open in <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{0,n+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigsqcup_{j}D_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>⨆</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigsqcup_{j}D_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is closed in this union. Then <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}</annotation></semantics></math> is naturally identified with the excision coboundary map</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex144" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex144.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\colon\bigoplus_{j}W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}(D_{j})\to W_{0}H^{\bullet+1}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.111em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\colon\bigoplus_{j}W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}(D_{j})\to W_{0}H^{\bullet+1}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS3.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">We must show that <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective. We will give a short algebraic proof of this surjectivity.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">To this end, it will be convenient to work with the <math id="S8.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-modules</span></p>
<table id="S8.Ex145" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex145.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{J}^{\star}_{0,n}:=J^{\star}_{0,n}\otimes\sgn_{n}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>sgn</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{J}^{\star}_{0,n}:=J^{\star}_{0,n}\otimes\sgn_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS3.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS3.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">Tensoring with <math id="S8.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sgn_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>sgn</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sgn_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be nicely incorporated in the sign conventions on graph complexes; we take generators for <math id="S8.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{J}^{\star}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{J}^{\star}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> to be graphs with a total ordering of all edges (not just the structural edges), and impose the relation that permuting the edges induces multiplication by the sign of the permutation. Decompose <math id="S8.SS3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{J}^{\star}_{0,n}\cong\tilde{V}_{0}\oplus\tilde{V}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>J</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>⊕</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{J}^{\star}_{0,n}\cong\tilde{V}_{0}\oplus\tilde{V}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, as above.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then we can identify the cohomology groups with subspaces of free Lie algebras</span></p>
<table id="S8.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex146"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex146.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H(\tilde{V}_{0},\tilde{\delta}_{split})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(\tilde{V}_{0},\tilde{\delta}_{split})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex146.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}\mathsf{Lie}(x_{1},\dots,\hat{x}_{j},\dots,x_{n})[-n]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖫𝗂𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}\mathsf{Lie}(x_{1},\dots,\hat{x}_{j},\dots,x_{n})[-n]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex146.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H(\tilde{V}_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>V</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H(\tilde{V}_{1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex146.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathsf{Lie}(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})[-n-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝖫𝗂𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathsf{Lie}(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})[-n-1]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS3.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">with <math id="S8.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Lie}(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖫𝗂𝖾</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Lie}(x_{1},\dots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> the part of the free Lie algebra with generators <math id="S8.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},\dots,x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},\dots,x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for which each generator appears exactly once. Here the identification between (trivalent) trees and Lie words is such that the root of our tree is taken to be <math id="S8.SS3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>, every vertex is replaced by one Lie bracket, and the <math id="S8.SS3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-labeled leaf is replaced by <math id="S8.SS3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, e.g.</span></p>
