<p id="S8.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let us filter <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\ast}_{1,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\ast}_{1,n}</annotation></semantics></math> by the number of <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations and consider the associated spectral sequence. On the <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>-page (the associated graded), the differential is <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math>, which preserves each of the six combinatorial types. So <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the direct sum of the homologies of the associated graph complexes, with respect to <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{split}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{split}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each term then has an evident interpretation in terms of weight zero cohomology of moduli spaces; for instance</p>
<table id="S8.Ex159" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex159.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(V_{1,0})\cong\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}W_{0}H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1})[-3]\quad\quad\mbox{ and }\quad\quad H(V_{2,1})\cong W_{0}H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+1})[-2]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≅</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mtext> and </mtext><mspace style="width:2em;" width="2em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(V_{1,0})\cong\bigoplus_{j=1}^{n}W_{0}H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{0,n+1})[-3]\quad\quad\mbox{ and }\quad\quad H(V_{2,1})\cong W_{0}H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{1,n+1})[-2].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Note that the cohomology of <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{i,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{i,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is supported in degree <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+i+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+i+k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The differential on <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the part of <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{join}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{join}</annotation></semantics></math> that reduces the number of <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations by exactly 1. This further decomposes as two parts, one that eliminates an <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,j)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge, and one that decreases the number of other <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations by <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, the <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page is the total complex of a diagram of the following form:</p>
<table id="S8.E40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="0" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(40)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S8.E40.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:427.3pt;height:60.5pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
