<p id="S8.SS4.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS4.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">The action of the morphism <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> on this cocycle is trivial since there are no <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-legs. Hence <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is also a cocycle in <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{g,1},\tilde{\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{g,1},\tilde{\delta})</annotation></semantics></math> and in <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
From (<a href="#S5.E33" title="In 3rd item ‣ 5.3. Completing the zig-zag ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>) on p. <a href="#S5.E33" title="In 3rd item ‣ 5.3. Completing the zig-zag ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">33</span></a>, we see that the corresponding class in <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained, up to a sign, by decorating the unique leg in <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> with a <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-class. We claim that this is the graphical representation of multiplying with a <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-class at the marking in <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math>. To see this, note that <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\bullet}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\bullet}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> via cup product, and recall that the weight spectral sequence abutting to <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>∙</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr H^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by a natural filtration on the sheaf of smooth differential forms with logarithmic poles along the boundary of <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, Chapter 4, Section 3]</cite>. It is related via Poincaré duality to the spectral sequence that we study here, abutting to <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is likewise induced by a natural filtration on currents. The <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-class is of pure weight 2 in <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{2}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math> and is represented by a smooth and closed differential form <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> without logarithmic poles. The cup product <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\cup\colon\gr_{\bullet}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\to\gr_{\bullet+2}H_{c}^{\bullet+2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">∪</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\cup\colon\gr_{\bullet}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})\to\gr_{\bullet+2}H_{c}^{\bullet+2}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore induced by the action of <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> on currents, which also induces a compatible action on every term on every page in our weight spectral sequence. The <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>-pullback of <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> is the corresponding <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-class on <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it follows that <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi\cup" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>∪</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi\cup</annotation></semantics></math> is induced by multiplication by <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> on every term of <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The claim follows by descending from <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}^{2}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m36" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and then lifting to <math id="S8.SS4.p14.m37" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}^{2}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This finishes the proof of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm6" title="Theorem 1.6. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.6</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS4.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS4.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS4.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">To show Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a> we consider again the injection from (<a href="#S8.E49" title="In Proof of Theorems  and . ‣ 8.4. The cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                
                J
                ⋆
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  1
                  ,
                  n ‣ 8. Direct summands with marked points ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">49</span></a>) to <math id="S8.SS4.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H_{c}^{\bullet}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S8.SS4.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 8.4. ‣ 8.3. The cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                
                J
                ⋆
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  0
                  ,
                  n ‣ 8. Direct summands with marked points ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a> directly produces the first summand in the formula of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>, and Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 8.6. ‣ 8.4. The cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                
                J
                ⋆
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  1
                  ,
                  n ‣ 8. Direct summands with marked points ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.6</span></a> produces the second summand.
The assertion on the dimension of <math id="S8.SS4.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔹</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is shown in Lemma <a href="#S8.Thmthm7" title="Lemma 8.7. ‣ 8.4. The cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                
                J
                ⋆
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  1
                  ,
                  n ‣ 8. Direct summands with marked points ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.7</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.Thmthm8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_rem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmthm8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 8.8</span></span><span id="S8.Thmthm8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmthm8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmthm8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmthm6" title="Proposition 8.6. ‣ 8.4. The cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                
                J
                ⋆
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  1
                  ,
                  n ‣ 8. Direct summands with marked points ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.6</span></a> yields an additional summand to <math id="S8.Thmthm8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>gr</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gr_{2}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,n})</annotation></semantics></math> corresponding to the subspace</p>
<table id="S8.Ex174" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S8.Ex174.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{A}_{n}[-n-1]\otimes\sgn_{n})\subset H(J^{\star}_{1,n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>sgn</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{A}_{n}[-n-1]\otimes\sgn_{n})\subset H(J^{\star}_{1,n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.Thmthm8.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Lacking lower bounds on the dimension of <math id="S8.Thmthm8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{A}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔸</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{A}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, we omitted this summand from the statement of Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S8.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">8.4.1. </span>Examples</h4>

<div id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let us illustrate Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a> with pictures of the simplest non-trivial cohomology classes that it provides. First, consider the first summand in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm7" title="Theorem 1.7. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.7</span></a> for <math id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)=(3,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)=(3,2)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This summand has dimension 1, concentrated in degree 8. A generating cocycle in <math id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{\star}_{3,2},\tilde{\delta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{\star}_{3,2},\tilde{\delta})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the linear combination</p>
<table id="S8.Ex175" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
