<p id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here the component with internal vertices is the generator of <math id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{6}(G^{(3,1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>6</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{6}(G^{(3,1)})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is isomorphic to <math id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="sW_{0}H_{c}^{6}({\mathcal{M}}_{3,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>6</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">sW_{0}H_{c}^{6}({\mathcal{M}}_{3,1})</annotation></semantics></math>. The disjoint union of two isolated edges corresponds to the generator of <math id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(J^{\star}_{0,2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(J^{\star}_{0,2})</annotation></semantics></math> of Example <a href="#S8.Thmthm5" title="Example 8.5. ‣ 8.3. The cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                
                J
                ⋆
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  0
                  ,
                  n ‣ 8. Direct summands with marked points ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.5</span></a>. (Note that <math id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H(J^{\star}_{0,2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H(J^{\star}_{0,2})</annotation></semantics></math> has dimension <math id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>, by Proposition <a href="#S8.Thmthm4" title="Proposition 8.4. ‣ 8.3. The cohomology of 
          
            
              
              
                
                J
                ⋆
              
              
                
                  
                  
                  
                
                
                  0
                  ,
                  n ‣ 8. Direct summands with marked points ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8.4</span></a>.)
The corresponding cocycle in <math id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{\star}_{3,2},\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{\star}_{3,2},\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by applying the involution <math id="S8.SS4.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math>. Up to a conventional overall sign that we omit, it is:</p>
