<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>A direct map <math id="A1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have connected the complexes <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> through a zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms. Here, we construct a direct map
<math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="F\colon X_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\colon X_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
that fits into a homotopy commutative triangle with the maps in the zig-zag.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The map <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is useful for giving geometrically meaningful representatives in <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of the cohomology classes we have constructed in <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmthm3" title="Theorem 3.3. ‣ 3.5. The quotient 
          
            
              ¯
              GK ‣ 3. The weight 2 Getzler-Kapranov graph complex ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, for <math id="A1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)\neq(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)\neq(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the projection <math id="A1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a quasi-isomorphism. Hence each cocycle in <math id="A1.p2.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> lifts to a cocycle on <math id="A1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, uniquely up to exact terms. We now turn to the construction of the map <math id="A1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, which we define initially as a map of graded vector spaces.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a generator for <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> structural edges.
First, suppose <math id="A1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has no <math id="A1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations. At least one of the <math id="A1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations must be adjacent to an internal vertex. If both <math id="A1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations are adjacent to internal vertices, then <math id="A1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by joining the <math id="A1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ω</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega</annotation></semantics></math>-legs to form a new edge, and decorating either half-edge by <math id="A1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, in the antisymmetric linear combination:</p>
