</div>
<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We begin by constructing a degree <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> map of graded vector spaces</span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex183" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex183.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="h\colon\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\colon\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p7.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p7.2.1" class="ltx_text">and show that it satisfies the required equation (<a href="#A1.E52" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>) to give a chain homotopy from <math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\circ\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\circ\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>. We will then use <math id="A1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> to show that <math id="A1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a map of complexes, and thereby prove the proposition.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> be a generator for <math id="A1.p8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We define <math id="A1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> by a local replacement at the special vertex as follows.
Suppose the special vertex of <math id="A1.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is decorated by <math id="A1.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is not joined to a leg, so the half-edge is joined to a half-edge at an internal vertex <math id="A1.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we let <math id="A1.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph obtained by merging the special vertex with <math id="A1.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, and decorating the new special vertex by <math id="A1.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{2}\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{2}\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of half-edges incident to <math id="A1.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Pictorially:</span></p>
