<p id="A1.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">with <math id="A1.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\cdots)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\cdots)</annotation></semantics></math> representing terms killed by <math id="A1.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(-)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">−</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(-)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Next, suppose that the special vertex of <math id="A1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is decorated by <math id="A1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> a half-edge incident to the special vertex. Then, <math id="A1.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is once again the class in <math id="A1.p11.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> represented by the decorated graph <math id="A1.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. If the half-edge <math id="A1.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> connects to a leg, then <math id="A1.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\Phi(\Gamma))=\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\Phi(\Gamma))=\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h(\Gamma)=h(\delta\Gamma)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(\Gamma)=h(\delta\Gamma)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, so (<a href="#A1.E52" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>) holds.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">If the half-edge <math id="A1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> points towards an internal vertex, then <math id="A1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\Phi(\Gamma))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\Phi(\Gamma))</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained by anti-symmetrizing over the two <math id="A1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-decorations one can put on the <math id="A1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-edge,</span></p>
