{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{{{{{}}{}{}{}{}{{}}}}}{{}}{}{}{}\lxSVG@stroke\lxSVG@drawpath@unclipped{M 14.31 13.52 L 3.09 26.89}{fill:none} \lx@inpgf@ignorespaces
\lxSVG@closescope }}}
\lxSVG@closescope {\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}{\lx@inpgf@ignorespaces}\hss}\lxSVG@discardpath\lxSVG@closescope \hss}}\lxSVG@closescope\endpgfpicture}}\right)=h(\delta\Gamma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p13.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p13.3.1" class="ltx_text">This completes the proof of (<a href="#A1.E52" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text">Having proved (<a href="#A1.E52" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">52</span></a>), we have that for all <math id="A1.p14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma\in\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in\widetilde{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math></span></p>
<table id="A1.Ex190" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex190.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta F(\Phi(\Gamma))-F(\delta\Phi(\Gamma))=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta F(\Phi(\Gamma))-F(\delta\Phi(\Gamma))=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p14.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p14.2.1" class="ltx_text">By Proposition <a href="#S5.Thmthm5" title="Proposition 5.5. ‣ 5.3. Completing the zig-zag ‣ 5. A zig-zag of quasi-isomorphisms of graph complexes ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.5</span></a>, the map <math id="A1.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is surjective, and hence <math id="A1.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\circ F=F\circ\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\circ F=F\circ\delta</annotation></semantics></math>. We conclude that <math id="A1.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a map of chain complexes, and <math id="A1.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is chain homotopy from <math id="A1.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\circ\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\circ\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, as required.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1. </span>The pullback map</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that the projection <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}\to{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is proper and extends to a morphism on the stable curves compactifications, also denoted <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall also that the boundary divisors <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}\smallsetminus{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}\smallsetminus{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}\smallsetminus{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>∖</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}:=\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}\smallsetminus{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> have normal crossings. Applying the “fundamental simplicial constructions” as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, §<span id="A1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">II</span>.I]</cite> gives an induced morphism <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> from the simplicial resolution of the constant sheaf <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> to that of the constant sheaf <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, induced by the normal crossings structure. After realizing the compactly supported cohomology of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> as the reduced cohomology of the mapping cones for the inclusions <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g,1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial{\mathcal{M}}_{g}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, we get an induced pullback morphism between the weight spectral sequences that abuts to <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\colon\gr H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\to\gr H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>gr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>gr</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\colon\gr H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g})\to\gr H_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{g,1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We consider the graphical interpretation of the the induced map on the <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-page of the weight spectral sequence, i.e., the induced map between the Getzler-Kapranov graph complexes. We claim that
<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\colon\mathsf{GK}_{g,0}\to\mathsf{GK}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\colon\mathsf{GK}_{g,0}\to\mathsf{GK}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>
is given by the formula</p>
<table id="A1.E53" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(53)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E53.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\Gamma=\sum_{v}p_{v}(\Gamma)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>v</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\Gamma=\sum_{v}p_{v}(\Gamma),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the sum is over all vertices of the graph <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\in\mathsf{GK}_{g,0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in\mathsf{GK}_{g,0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> by attaching a leg labeled by <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to vertex <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. If the old decoration of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in H^{i}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{h,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in H^{i}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{h,k})</annotation></semantics></math>, then the new decoration on the vertex is given by <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}\alpha\in H^{i}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{h,k+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\alpha\in H^{i}(\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{h,k+1})</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi\colon\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{h,k+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{h,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi\colon\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{h,k+1}\to\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{h,k}</annotation></semantics></math> forgetting the new marking. To see this, note that if <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> has <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> edges then the underlying graphs of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{v}(\Gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{v}(\Gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, obtained by attaching a leg labeled <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> to a vertex <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, correspond precisely to the strata of codimension <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> that map onto the codimension <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> stratum <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{\Gamma}({\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\subset\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{\Gamma}({\mathcal{M}}_{\Gamma})\subset\overline{{\mathcal{M}}}_{\Gamma}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the operation on decorations that we have described corresponds, via the Künneth decomposition, to the induced pullback on the cohomology of strata in the simplicial resolution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The same formula (<a href="#A1.E53" title="In A.1. The pullback map ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">53</span></a>) also defines a pullback operation</p>
<table id="A1.Ex191" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex191.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi^{*}\colon\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,0}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,1}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}\colon\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,0}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and the projection map <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝖦𝖪</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GK}_{g,n}\to\overline{\mathsf{GK}}{}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> intertwines the two operations <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We furthermore define another operation</p>
<table id="A1.Ex192" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex192.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi^{*}\colon(H_{g},\tilde{\delta})\to(X^{\star}_{g,1},\tilde{\delta})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>δ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi^{*}\colon(H_{g},\tilde{\delta})\to(X^{\star}_{g,1},\tilde{\delta})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">by taking minus the disjoint union with an <math id="A1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge,</p>
<table id="A1.Ex193" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
