<div id="A1.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof sketch.</h6>
<div id="A1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">A graph <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\in H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> contains either no <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-leg, or exactly two, on an <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge.
We consider both cases in turn.
Suppose first that <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\in H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> contains no <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-leg.
Then its image under the first two horizontal maps in (<a href="#A1.E54" title="In Lemma A.2. ‣ A.1. The pullback map ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>) is just the same unaltered graph <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> produces the linear combination (<a href="#A1.E50" title="In Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">50</span></a>). Applying <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> afterwards produces a linear combination of graphs of the form</span></p>
