<p id="A1.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">In the first two sums one sums over all ways of attaching the <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-labeled leg to a vertex, including the special vertex.
Here we are using <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, Proposition 3.1 (ii)]</cite> to compute the pullback of the <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-class, and the additional <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{0,S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{0,S}</annotation></semantics></math>-terms there produce the last two summands.
To compare, let us now follow our graph <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math> along the lower rim of (<a href="#A1.E54" title="In Lemma A.2. ‣ A.1. The pullback map ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>).
The vertical arrow <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varpi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϖ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varpi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> adds one <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,1)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge to <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Call the resulting graph <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\Gamma^{\prime}\in X^{\star}_{g,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\Gamma^{\prime}\in X^{\star}_{g,1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> then produces <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> plus the sum of all graphs obtained by attaching a <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-labeled leg to <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> produces from this latter sum the two sums in (<a href="#A1.E55" title="In Proof sketch. ‣ A.1. The pullback map ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>).
Applying <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> produces the last two summands in (<a href="#A1.E55" title="In Proof sketch. ‣ A.1. The pullback map ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">55</span></a>) via (<a href="#A1.E51" title="In Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">51</span></a>).
This shows commutativity of (<a href="#A1.E54" title="In Lemma A.2. ‣ A.1. The pullback map ‣ Appendix A A direct map 
        
          X
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          →
          
            ¯
            GK
          
          
            
              
                
                
                
              
              
                g
                ,
                n
              
            
          
          
            2 ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">54</span></a>) for <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\in H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> without <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge.
The proof in the case where <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma\in H_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\in H_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> has an <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge is essentially similar, although the map <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Xi</annotation></semantics></math> produces more terms in presence of an <math id="A1.SS1.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>-edge.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Numerical results</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We record the cohomology groups of the graph complexes <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{(g,n)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{(g,n)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J^{\star}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>⋆</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J^{\star}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with the characters of their <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-representations for small <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, obtained by calculations in Sage. For example, the entry <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t^{11}s_{2,1}+t^{12}s_{1,1,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>11</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>12</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t^{11}s_{2,1}+t^{12}s_{1,1,1}</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#A2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Appendix B Numerical results ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,n)=(4,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,n)=(4,3)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates that <math id="A2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{11}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{4,3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>11</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{11}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{4,3})</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional irreducible representation of <math id="A2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{S}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝕊</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{S}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> with character <math id="A2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2,1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{12}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{4,3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mn>12</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{12}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{4,3})</annotation></semantics></math> is the sign representation, and <math id="A2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{4,3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>∙</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{0}H^{\bullet}_{c}({\mathcal{M}}_{4,3})</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes in all other degrees. The full cohomology of <math id="A2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{g,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is well-understood for <math id="A2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="A2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{0,n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperplane arrangement complement, and also for <math id="A2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. For <math id="A2.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we understand that Tommasi has computed the cohomology of <math id="A2.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{M}}_{2,n}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="A2.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, cf. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> for weight 0 and <math id="A2.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. The additional computations in Figure <a href="#A2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Appendix B Numerical results ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> were previously known as <math id="A2.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℚ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}</annotation></semantics></math>-vector spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, Appendix A]</cite>. We include the details here to facilitate comparison with the computations in Figures <a href="#A2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Appendix B Numerical results ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Appendix B Numerical results ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. To the best of our understanding, the computations in Figure <a href="#A2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Appendix B Numerical results ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> are new for <math id="A2.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, as are all of the computations in Figure <a href="#A2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Appendix B Numerical results ‣ Weight two compactly supported cohomology of moduli spaces of curves" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
